WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.470
- Good afternoon everyone.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.283
I'm Peter Salovey, the
President of Yale University.

00:00:12.181 --> 00:00:17.181
I--
(audience applauding)

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.840
You know, I love the fact that that line

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.840
still gathers applause, you know?

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.873
There will come a day when it won't.

00:00:25.775 --> 00:00:27.080
(Peter laughs)

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.040
Well on behalf of the
trustees of Yale University,

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.760
the faculty, and everyone here at Yale

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.440
and in the New Haven community,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.350
it's my honor to introduce
Ambassador Samantha Power.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:44.030
Ambassador Power is here on
campus today as a Chubb Fellow.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.890
Hendon Chubb was a 1895 graduate

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.670
of Yale's Sheffield Scientific School

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.870
and in 1936 he made a substantial
gift to the University

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.410
to encourage our students to

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.303
take an interest in public service.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.010
Since 1949 his endowment has been used

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.540
to underwrite a prestigious
visiting speakers program

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.500
bringing extraordinary
public figures to the campus.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.010
The Fellowship is
administered by the Master

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.190
of Timothy Dwight College, who
is currently Jeffrey Brenzel,

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.240
who you will see later in the program.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.407
(audience applauding)

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.590
Past Chubb Fellows have included

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.080
four presidents of the United States,

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.970
presidents of eight other countries,

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.780
and more than 150 other
regionally, nationally,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.100
and internationally prominent individuals

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.483
noted for their distinction
in service to the common good.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.970
I'm especially delighted to introduce

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.620
Ambassador Power today for two reasons.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.400
First we are celebrating her homecoming.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.890
She earned her bachelor's degree

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.130
in history from Yale in 1992

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.702
and I found that one of
the great pleasures--

00:01:51.702 --> 00:01:56.702
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.810
I don't know if those are
all the history majors

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.682
or the class of 1992 but,

00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:02.140
(audience laughing)

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.580
we found one of the greatest pleasures

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.460
and privileges in the Yale presidency

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.830
is the opportunity to
welcome back graduates

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.000
who have distinguished
themselves in the world.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.600
In this particular case I confess

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.090
to having a personal stake in the outcome.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.312
Ambassador Power was a student
in my psychology class.

00:02:18.312 --> 00:02:19.810
(audience laughing)

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.320
I don't know whether she
has been able to apply

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.870
anything that she actually
learned in that class

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.930
in the years since but I'm happy now,

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:31.310
perhaps happier that she
might have been at the time

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:32.880
to make careful note of the fact

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.320
that she was one of my students.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.520
The second reason it is a special pleasure

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.620
to welcome Ambassador Power today

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.310
is that collectively Yale
owes her a debt of gratitude.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.170
I would put it this way,

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.090
she has chosen to do with her life

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.970
what we aspire to prepare
all of our students to do.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.980
She has taken maximal advantage,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.620
both here and elsewhere,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.490
of the privileged
educational opportunities

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:02.670
that her capacities and
circumstances presented to her

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:04.980
and she has fully embraced
these opportunities

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.620
in order to accomplish
extraordinary things,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.610
both with respect to the life of the mind

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.400
and to the life of the world.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.180
Most of you know that Ambassador Power

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.250
has combined in an
unusual and powerful way

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.863
the careers of an academic
and a public servant.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.340
While in law school at Harvard
she began writing a paper

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.360
that became her Pulitzer Prize winning,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.290
and immensely influential book,

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.920
A Problem from Hell: America
and the Age of Genocide,

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.620
published in 2002.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.120
It has been said of this book

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.000
that it influenced more young people

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.140
and in particular more women

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.430
to devote themselves to studying
and working in human rights

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:49.370
than any work published before or since.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:50.620
In an often told story,

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.560
we know that the book also moved

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.040
the then Junior Senator
from Illinois, Barack Obama,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.700
to meet her for dinner.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.020
From there her story accelerates.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.270
Staff member to Senator Obama,

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:05.660
Foreign Policy Advisor to
the presidential campaign,

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.360
Special Assistant to the President,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.510
and Senior Director for
Multilateral Affairs

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.170
and Human Rights,

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.160
US Permanent Representative
to the United Nations,

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.220
the youngest-ever at age 43,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.560
and throughout her rapid rise
to positions of accountability

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.350
and influence, Samantha
Power has demonstrated

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:26.710
a remarkable commitment to probing

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:28.750
the often troubling relationship

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:30.850
between the prerogatives of power

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:33.060
and the obligations of power.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.620
Here at Yale we are grateful and honored

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.140
to have her as a member of our community,

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.350
where once she came to
study in our classrooms,

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.900
today she has returned to offer us

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.670
her own instruction and wisdom.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:49.850
Please join me in extending to her

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:52.640
our own warm welcome back to Yale.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:53.610
Ambassador Power.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:58.610
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:04:59.446 --> 00:05:00.279
Thank you so much

00:05:00.279 --> 00:05:01.112
for doing this.
- Happy to do it.

00:05:01.112 --> 00:05:02.744
So nice of you to join us.
- Thank you.

00:05:02.744 --> 00:05:05.994
(audience applauding)

00:05:14.931 --> 00:05:16.270
This is not the welcome I get

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:19.015
when I walk into the UN
Security Council every morning.

00:05:19.015 --> 00:05:21.730
(audience laughing)

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:23.830
I should come here more often.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:25.490
It's wonderful, truly wonderful,

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.213
very moving for me to be back.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.130
I came to Yale from Atlanta Georgia.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.580
I went to a public high
school in Atlanta Georgia

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:39.160
and being here it is
no overstatement to say

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.500
that being here completely
changed my life.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.110
It was an amazing experience,

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.770
many of my teachers are
here, who I still think about

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.890
even as I do my day job.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:51.630
I went from wanting to be a sportscaster,

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.410
which would have been a very worthy career

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:58.732
and still I pine for it at times,

00:05:58.732 --> 00:05:59.790
(audience laughing)

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:01.890
to wanting to do something, anything,

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:03.830
in American foreign policy

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.750
because of the teaching
and the inspiration

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:09.060
that I got from the Yale faculty

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.020
and from my fellow classmates.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:14.490
I will say that I never
ever, ever, ever imagined

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.890
that I would come back to Yale

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.360
to speak as the UN Ambassador,
so it's thrilling for me.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.193
Thank you.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.810
We are living in a time,
it is very fair to say,

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:30.550
of daunting and perpetual crises.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:33.490
The deadly spread of Ebola in West Africa,

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:37.070
a string of shocking executions by ISIL,

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.890
more tanks and heavy armaments

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:41.570
rolling across Ukraine's border

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:43.980
to Russian backed separatists,

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.010
barrel bombs and chlorine being dropped

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.530
by the Assad's regime on Syrian civilians,

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:50.510
child soldiers being abducted

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.360
and forced to fight in a brutal civil war

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.550
in the world's newest
country, South Sudan,

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.330
the list goes on and on.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:01.570
There's so many crises that
when we turn our attention

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.140
to working to bring one
of these under control

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:08.450
or trying to reduce the suffering

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:11.693
that a particular crisis
is causing two more pop up.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.320
At a different time in our history

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:20.320
we may have felt tempted to
ignore some of these threats,

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:23.300
we may have thought that
we wouldn't pay a price.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:27.320
In 1776 Thomas Paine wrote that, quote,

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.880
America, remote from all
the wrangling of the world

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.430
may live at ease, bounded by the ocean

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:35.910
and backed by the wilderness,

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:40.370
what hath she to fear
but her God, end quote.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:43.290
But in today's interconnected
world we don't have the luxury

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:46.130
of remoteness from the world's wrangling.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:48.010
It can impact us more swiftly

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:50.920
and more deeply than ever before.

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:53.290
Now this is not news to any of you,

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:56.730
you see it, you feel
it, you understand that

00:07:56.730 --> 00:08:00.090
America cannot afford
to ignore these threats

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:01.660
and at the same time you understand that

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:05.330
today's threats are so
immense and so complex

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:08.160
that even if the United
States wanted to tackle

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.530
any one of them alone, we
would not be able to do so.

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:16.050
We need to rally the international
community to our side.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:18.900
The point I want to make to you today

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.670
is that in addition to leading
the international community

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:25.710
in responding to these
threats in real time,

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:28.300
we need to help shape a
world where such threats

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:31.940
are like less likely to
arise in the first place.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:34.520
This sounds so obvious, I know,

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.810
and yet generations of policymakers

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:40.440
across the political spectrum
have struggled to dedicate

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.750
the bandwidth, financial resources,

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.150
and diplomatic and political capital

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:49.740
to make these long-term investments.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.380
As someone who came into
government fully convinced

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.870
of the critical importance
of seeing beyond

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:56.550
the crisis of the moment,

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:59.230
I can tell you that it is
surprisingly challenging

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:02.470
in practice to do so, given all else.

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.940
But my message to you
today is that we cannot

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:09.400
treat the short and long term
as an either/or proposition.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.150
We have to try to put out a lot of fires

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.500
in the here and now,

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.820
but we can't let the
immediacy of today's crises

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.700
distract us from playing a smart long game

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.060
that will create more stability

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:21.630
and more security in the long run.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.640
Let me offer a few examples,
curbing the spread of Ebola,

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.210
protecting the rights of women and girls,

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.873
and countering violent extremism.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.870
On September 18th the
United States chaired

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:40.820
the first-ever emergency meeting
of the UN Security Council

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:43.500
on a public health crisis, Ebola.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.440
By that time more than 2,500
people had died of the virus

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:51.180
and some 5,500 had been reported infected

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:53.653
in Guinea, Liberia, and Sierra Leone.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.510
A Liberian health care
worker named Jackson Naimah,

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:02.060
who was working at Medecins
Sans Frontieres, Ebola Clinic

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:03.760
in the capital of Monrovia,

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.010
spoke to the council via video link.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.350
He described people
dying outside the gates

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:12.110
of the clinic where he worked

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:15.180
because there were just no
more beds to take them in.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.560
He said, quote, "I feel that
the future of my country

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:20.340
is hanging in the balance.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:23.050
If the international
community does not stand up

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:28.050
we will be wiped out," end quote.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:31.050
Now the United States has done
more than any other country

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:34.130
to help West Africa respond
to the Ebola crisis.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:37.610
The CDC deployed teams to
the region way back in March,

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.150
shortly after the outbreak began.

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:42.860
In July President Obama declared
controlling the epidemic

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:44.500
a national security priority

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:46.680
and most importantly days before

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.180
that September Security Council meeting

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:50.950
the president announced the largest

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:54.000
global health intervention in US history,

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:55.710
resulting so far in the deployment

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:59.020
of more than 3,000 US civilian
and military personnel

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:01.480
from the Department of Defense, US Aid,

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:03.870
the CDC, and other agencies.

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:05.310
We're supporting the construction

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.610
of 15 Ebola treatment
units, so called ETUs,

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.770
and brave and brilliant men and women

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:13.770
in the US Public Health
Service Commissioned Corps

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:17.410
provide Ebola treatment
at a 25-bed hospital

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:19.100
built by the US military

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.450
and dedicated to treating
healthcare workers

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:24.640
and Ebola responders
who contract the virus.

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:28.400
This ensures that medical
volunteers and UN personnel

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.300
from around the world will
receive top-notch care

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:32.840
if they are infected while working

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.360
on the front lines of this epidemic.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:37.020
The United States is training hundreds

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:38.310
of Liberian healthcare workers

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:40.840
at a newly built center in Monrovia,

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.130
and countless more through
mobile training teams

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:45.550
traveling to remote areas.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:49.580
But even with all this we know
that the United States alone

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:52.570
cannot curb the epidemic's deadly spread.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:56.260
That's why we mobilized
133 other countries,

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:58.600
the most in the history
of the United Nations

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:02.420
to co-sponsor a Security Council
resolution declaring Ebola

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:05.020
a threat to international
peace and security.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:06.990
It's why President Obama
personally launched

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.970
a full-court press to mobilize commitments

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.180
from other countries,

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:13.130
keeping heads of state around
the world on speed-dial

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.180
to convince them to do more,

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:17.550
and it is why we are now
holding other countries

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:19.990
to the commitments that they have made

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:22.550
and pressing those who
have committed too little

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:25.630
or who've committed nothing
at all to do their part

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:28.520
because we will not defeat Ebola with a

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:31.560
some hands on deck effort.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.600
Having visited the most affected countries

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:36.400
at the end of October I can
tell you that we are beginning

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:39.860
to see the impact of
our collective response.

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:42.130
In Bong County, Liberia I visited

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:45.070
a US Navy run Ebola testing lab,

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:46.930
which had cut the time local Liberians

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:49.860
waited for test results from five days

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.530
to between three and five hours.

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:56.370
On November fourth only 33 out of 53

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:59.630
Ebola affected districts across the region

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:02.370
had the ability to transport samples

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:05.760
to a lab within 24 hours
of collecting them,

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:07.670
according to the WHO.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:10.500
By November 17th, just 13 days later,

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:14.220
all 53 districts had that capability,

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:16.640
and lab testing time matters

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:18.230
because the sooner you get your results

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:20.460
the sooner you can leave facilities

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:21.407
where you might be waiting with people

00:13:21.407 --> 00:13:23.460
who are actually infected

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:26.220
and in many of these labs
about 70% of the results

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:28.180
are coming back negative,

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:30.760
so actually a large number of people

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:33.180
who are being tested are not
those who in fact have Ebola,

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:35.090
they may have malaria, they
may have some other ailment,

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:36.530
that allows them to leave

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:38.040
and it frees up bed space

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:41.150
for those who need to
be isolated with Ebola.

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:45.540
The results affirm what we have
said all along about Ebola,

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:47.570
which is that we know
how to win this fight.

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:50.120
We can bend Ebola's deadly curve

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:54.210
but bending that curve is
very different from ending it.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:56.190
It would be a huge
mistake to think that just

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:59.650
because we are seeing signs
of progress in some areas

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:03.210
that we are close to stopping
this outbreak, we are not.

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.330
In Sierra Leone the
disease is spreading faster

00:14:05.330 --> 00:14:08.900
than at any time since the outbreak began,

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:10.640
with more than 900 new infections

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:14.210
reported in the two-week period
that ended November 23rd.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:16.630
Some 16,000 people have been reported

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:18.570
infected in West Africa

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:21.570
and more than 5,500 people have died.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:25.230
The actual figures are likely
in fact to be much higher.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:27.970
Local contact tracing
teams outside of capitals,

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:30.870
which are critical to breaking
chains of transmission

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:32.700
continue to lack basic resources,

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:34.860
like gas for their motorcycles,

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:38.620
or even credit for their SIM
cards on their cell phones,

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:41.110
and the human toll is staggering.

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:44.640
Think for a second about
the heartbreak associated

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:47.840
with any one of the 4,000 boys and girls

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:52.480
who have lost a mother or father
to the virus, 4000 orphans,

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:55.450
or the incalculable loss
of warmth in countries

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:58.050
where people used to hug when they met

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.200
but now are told not to wipe the tears

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.230
from the eyes of a sick child

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:04.880
in case the child might be infected,

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:06.660
or the pain experienced by the people

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.350
who have beaten the virus,

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:11.950
only to find themselves
stigmatized or even run out of town

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:13.620
by their friends and their communities

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.070
when they attempt to go home.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:18.620
Yet in spite of all the suffering
the outbreak has caused,

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:20.270
and it is immense,

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.230
and in spite of the virus's surge

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.460
in parts of the affected countries,

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.770
we have to find a way to
look beyond the outbreak

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:32.020
and ask what factors made
these countries so vulnerable.

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:34.900
We have to take steps now
so that in the long term

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:38.690
we avoid finding ourselves
in this situation again.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:41.000
One reason Ebola spreads so fast was

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.150
because West Africa had never experienced

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:44.880
an Ebola outbreak before

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:46.820
and even before the virus surfaced

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:50.360
public health systems in Guinea,
Liberia, and Sierra Leone

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:52.410
were stretched beyond capacity

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:55.910
just to meet the day-to-day
needs of their citizens.

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:59.610
Prior to the outbreak Liberia
had approximately 50 doctors

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:02.810
for the entire country
of 4.3 million people.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:06.660
That's around one doctor
for every 100,000 people.

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:11.410
Sierra Leone had two doctors
for every 100,000 people.

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:15.650
The United States by
contrast has 245 doctors

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:17.950
for every 100,000 people.

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:21.450
The United Kingdom has 279, Germany 380.

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:24.460
Yale New Haven Hospital, which is just one

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:27.290
of nearly 50 hospitals
in Connecticut alone

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:30.493
has more than 600 resident physicians.

00:16:31.336 --> 00:16:33.963
Liberia had 50, before Ebola.

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:38.430
The Yale New Haven number is
12 times the number of doctors

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:41.540
in all of pre-Ebola Liberia.

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:43.210
The Yale School of Medicine graduates

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:46.900
over 10 times the number of
doctors in a single class

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:50.010
then worked in Liberia before Ebola began

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:53.580
taking some of the country's
best nurses and doctors.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:57.530
Ebola has killed approximately
330 healthcare workers

00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:00.560
in West Africa, many of
whom were not working

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:02.940
to treat Ebola when they were infected,

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.730
but rather were providing
routine medical assistance,

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:07.880
such as delivering babies.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:09.900
The affected countries also lacked

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:12.900
and in many instances continue to lack,

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:15.500
protective suits and even plastic gloves

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.310
to keep healthcare workers safe.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:20.360
They lack lab capacity,
emergency operation centers,

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:22.570
and disease surveillance systems.

00:17:22.570 --> 00:17:25.700
Communities were not aware
and some are still not aware

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:29.070
of what Ebola is or how it is spread.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:30.530
All of these deficiencies meant

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:33.300
that when the Ebola
outbreak hit these countries

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:36.840
it hit harder and spread faster.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:39.370
Leaders and communities in the region

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:42.630
understand the importance
of plugging these gaps.

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:45.930
They understand the dangerous,
short sightedness of

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:50.130
thinking our job is done when
we end the current outbreak.

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:53.420
They know what epidemiologists'
research proves,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:56.010
another outbreak whether of Ebola

00:17:56.010 --> 00:18:00.900
or of another deadly disease
like SARS or MERS is inevitable

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:02.870
but whether these outbreaks become

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:05.713
full-blown epidemics is up to us.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:10.640
When I met with President Ellen
Johnson Sirleaf in Liberia

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:11.640
on my trip to the region

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:14.000
and asked her what keeps her up at night,

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:16.680
she said it was the
country's restless youth.

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.700
Three-quarters of Liberia's population

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:21.170
is under the age of 35.

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:22.500
She said she worried about kids

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:23.750
who've been out of school for months,

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:26.520
about young men and women
who've lost their jobs

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:31.140
since Liberia's economy
started its ongoing decline.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:34.080
One out of every two
Liberians who was employed

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:37.280
at the beginning of the
outbreak is now out of work.

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:39.060
Seven in ten people surveyed

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:41.603
said they do not have
enough money to buy food.

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:43.740
President Conde of Guinea

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:47.200
and President Koroma of Sierra
Leone voice similar concerns.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:51.160
So did so many of the community
and civil society leaders

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:53.370
I met with in all three countries.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:57.690
They were worried that
making it to, Ebola free

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.220
will quickly translate
into these countries

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:02.630
being assistance free.

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:04.510
As we in the international community

00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:07.370
quickly pack up and leave behind countries

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:10.800
with far greater insecurity
and far weaker economies

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:12.790
with thousands of orphans children

00:19:12.790 --> 00:19:15.740
and public institutions in shambles.

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:17.760
Recognizing these acute risks,

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:22.410
we are now looking beyond ending
this Ebola epidemic's curve

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:26.500
to preventing the next
infectious disease epidemic.

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:29.730
Back in February, before
the first cases of Ebola

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.320
in Guinea came to light,

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:32.670
the United States launched the

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:36.130
Global Health Security Agenda, the GHSA.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:38.150
The Agenda's aimed at preparing countries

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:41.800
to prevent, detect, and
rapidly respond to outbreaks

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:43.700
before they become epidemics.

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:45.800
During a meeting with
President Obama in September

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:47.980
43 countries joined us in announcing

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:50.920
over 100 specific
commitments to strengthen

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:53.730
global health security,
together with the WHO

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.170
and other international institutions.

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:59.160
The GHSA is predicated
on the understanding

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:01.330
that in a world where an outbreak

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:03.670
that begins in the forests of Guinea

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.890
can quickly spread across
borders, continents,

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:09.340
and even oceans, a
weakness in any part of our

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:12.530
interconnected global
health security system

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:14.600
is a threat to us all.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:17.110
We and our partners have
set out 11 areas of focus

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:20.550
to strengthen national,
regional, and global capacity.

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:22.620
These include boosting immunization rates

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:24.980
to stop the resurgence
of preventable diseases,

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:27.780
ensuring countries have
nationwide lab systems,

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:30.660
capable of conducting rapid accurate tests

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:32.840
to detect outbreaks early,

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:34.950
and developing and
implementing the real time

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:36.450
disease surveillance systems

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:39.630
and the workforce required
to track outbreaks,

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:42.550
like the contact tracing
teams that are so critical

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:45.550
to tracking down those who
might have had physical contact

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:47.660
with someone infected with Ebola.

00:20:47.660 --> 00:20:50.100
Each area focused within the GHSA

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.680
has a five year implementation plan,

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:54.700
which sets out concrete
benchmarks along the way,

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.720
and a way to measure them.

00:20:56.720 --> 00:20:59.800
To meet crises head-on where they are,

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:02.900
we are also developing
new regional partners,

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:06.870
such as the African Union's own
Centers for Disease Control,

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:11.020
which the AU and the United
States agreed to jointly create

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:14.140
during this year's
US-Africa Leaders Summit

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:18.020
and our own CDC is helping
to advise and build.

00:21:18.020 --> 00:21:20.800
We're also investing in
the young policymakers,

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:22.900
innovators, and civil society leaders

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:25.340
who will lead their
countries in responding,

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:28.700
not only to this kind of
crisis but other crises.

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:30.350
Whether inside or outside of government

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:33.030
these individuals will
do the yeoman's work

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:34.960
of shoring up institutions

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:36.880
and making societies more resilient

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:40.500
and less vulnerable to global
threats like epidemics.

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.440
This is why President Obama launched

00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:44.840
the Young African Leaders Initiative,

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.170
which brought its first class of

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:49.930
500 Mandela Washington
fellows to the United States

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:51.980
to study this past summer.

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:54.240
20 of our nation's top universities,

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:56.600
including Yale, I'm proud to say,

00:21:56.600 --> 00:21:58.430
hosted Mandela Washington fellows

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:01.300
for six weeks of leadership
training, skill building,

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:03.630
and networking with their peers.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:05.480
Some of you in the audience
may have had the chance

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:09.640
to hear from these fellows
about their remarkable work,

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:13.370
often done in the most
daunting circumstances.

00:22:13.370 --> 00:22:16.620
One of the Mandela Washington
fellows in this year's class

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:20.140
was Sarah McGill, a 28 year old Liberian.

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:24.140
When Sarah returned home this
fall, she noticed a problem

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:26.860
because of the exponential spread of Ebola

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:28.740
when Sarah went shopping in Monrovia

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:30.990
all the stores had signs
outside telling people

00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:34.690
to wash their hands in
chlorinated water before entering.

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:37.300
Most shops had a five liter bucket outside

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.220
and at the busier businesses

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:41.400
the wait could take up to ten minutes.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:44.630
During that time people
crowded into long lines

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:47.220
when they were at greater
risk of infection.

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:51.110
Others gave up and left
costing the stores business.

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:54.830
So Sarah designed a simple 440 liter tank

00:22:54.830 --> 00:22:56.330
with six individual stations,

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:58.950
which permit multiple people
to wash their hands at once

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:01.260
and 'cause the tank is much larger,

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:03.040
it only needs to be refilled once a day

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:05.540
as opposed to every few
hours with smaller buckets.

00:23:05.540 --> 00:23:08.860
16 communities are now
using this simple tank

00:23:08.860 --> 00:23:11.580
that Sarah designed and
more plan to build them,

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:13.360
particularly outside of schools,

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:16.100
enabling potentially
schools to open back up

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:19.080
and the necessary precautions
to be put in place.

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.800
Investing in a problem solver like Sarah

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:23.680
and all of the young problem solvers

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:27.870
in this audience tonight, has
to be part of our long game.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:31.770
Now I've been on campus for
about two and a half hours

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:34.800
and I've already gotten
an administration's worth

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.230
of policy recommendations today

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:38.230
from Yale students and faculty.

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:39.180
Thank you for that,

00:23:40.510 --> 00:23:41.897
but let me offer one to you

00:23:41.897 --> 00:23:44.800
and to take advantage of the leadership

00:23:44.800 --> 00:23:46.460
who is present here today.

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:50.010
I would urge you not only
to host future classes

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:51.610
of Mandela Washington fellows,

00:23:51.610 --> 00:23:54.960
but also to deepen your
engagement with the initiative

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.100
by sending Yale students, faculty members

00:23:57.100 --> 00:23:59.320
and researchers out into the field

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:03.140
to partner with the fellows
who have studied here.

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:06.310
Dartmouth, which also
hosted fellows in 2014,

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:08.160
has started a program like this

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:10.350
and its first batch of
students are on their way to

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:13.330
Tanzania, Ghana, Angola, and Zimbabwe

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:15.640
to work with fellows this winter.

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:18.400
Of course Ebola is not the only crisis

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:20.097
where it is in America's interests

00:24:20.097 --> 00:24:22.490
and the world's to play the long game,

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:25.300
even as we respond to immediate threats.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:29.060
Another is advancing the
rights of women and girls.

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:31.390
We've got to address the immediate crisis

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:33.810
of widespread gender-based violence,

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:36.490
particularly in wartime,
while at the same time

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:39.230
making the crucial long-term investments

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:41.890
in the empowerment of women and girls.

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:43.790
Violence against women during conflicts

00:24:43.790 --> 00:24:45.890
is anything but a new problem.

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:47.270
One of the earliest accounts

00:24:47.270 --> 00:24:49.410
of the mass abduction of women and girls

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:52.400
can be found in Plutarch's Parallel Lives,

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:54.470
where the seizure of Sabine women

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:57.760
played a central part
in the creation of Rome.

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:01.480
Plutarch wrote, armed with swords then,

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:03.330
many of Romulus's followers

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:05.640
kept their eyes intently upon him

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:08.660
and when the signal was
given drew their swords,

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:10.440
rushed in with shouts,

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:14.290
and ravished away the
daughters of the Sabines.

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:16.980
Some say that only 30 maidens were seized

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:19.800
and that from these the curiae were named,

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:23.920
but Valerius Antaeus
puts the number at 527

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:27.793
and Juba at 683, all maidens.

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:31.380
We know that violence
against women and girls

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:34.580
in conflicts persists
with disturbing frequency,

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:35.750
yet the statistics

00:25:35.750 --> 00:25:39.640
and the horrifying individual
acts continue to shock.

00:25:39.640 --> 00:25:42.150
Consider the Democratic Republic of Congo

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:44.130
where sexual violence has long been used

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:47.630
as a tactic of war in the
decades-long conflict.

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:49.420
In one of the most devastating incidents,

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:54.420
at least 387 people including
300 women and 55 girls

00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:58.680
were raped by armed groups in
13 villages in Eastern Congo

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:02.330
between July 30th and August 2nd, 2010,

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:03.563
just a three-day period.

00:26:04.610 --> 00:26:06.860
While conflicts exacerbate such violence

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:08.500
it is a daily fact of life

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:11.220
for women and girls around the world.

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:13.080
One in three women is the victim

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:16.380
of physical or sexual
violence in their lifetimes.

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:20.150
The UN estimates that 150
million girls worldwide

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:22.490
experienced sexual violence each year.

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:24.280
In Sao Paulo, Brazil's largest city

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:29.130
a woman is assaulted every 15
seconds, according to UN Women

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:32.540
and in many places the problem
is getting worse, not better.

00:26:32.540 --> 00:26:34.660
In Honduras the number of
violent deaths of women

00:26:34.660 --> 00:26:39.530
increased by 263% from 2005 to 2013,

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:42.210
yet in 95% of sexual violence crimes

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:44.460
and killings of women in Honduras,

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:47.010
the perpetrators are never punished.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:48.940
There are immediate steps we can take

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:51.400
to stop violence against women and girls,

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:53.920
both during conflicts and in peacetime.

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:56.527
We can improve accountability
for violence against women

00:26:56.527 --> 00:27:00.200
and girls through strengthening
documentation of abuses

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:02.540
and the capacity of justice officials

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:04.620
to prosecute perpetrators.

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:05.700
Physicians for Human Rights

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:08.410
created a mobile app, MediCapt,

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:10.460
which it piloted this year in the Congo

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:14.450
and which converts standardized
medical intake forms into

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:18.780
a way to gather forensic
documentation for prosecutions.

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:21.570
When a medical professional
fills out the form using the app

00:27:21.570 --> 00:27:23.660
it is immediately turned
into a digital record

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:26.810
which can be used by
prosecutors to trace patterns

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:29.950
and build cases against perpetrators.

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:32.080
The app can be used with a digital camera,

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:33.990
allowing medical
professionals to supplement

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:37.520
their written reports with
photographs of key evidence,

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:39.610
such as physical bruising.

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:42.700
For this evidence to be useful,
countries need prosecutors

00:27:42.700 --> 00:27:45.750
who are capable of building
cases upon such evidence

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:47.780
and judges with the independence

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:50.280
to punish powerful perpetrators,

00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:53.090
yet these are basic institutions
that many countries,

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:56.450
including the Democratic
Republic of Congo, still lack

00:27:56.450 --> 00:27:58.810
and this is why in June Secretary Kerry

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:01.320
announced a new accountability initiative

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:04.440
to support specialized
justice sector mechanisms,

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:07.790
such as mobile courts, to
improve access to justice for

00:28:07.790 --> 00:28:11.590
survivors of sexual and
gender-based violence in conflicts

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:13.750
and to help build the judicial capacity

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:15.103
of partner governments.

00:28:16.270 --> 00:28:19.040
As you all know, the
struggle for accountability

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:21.910
must not only be waged in conflict zones,

00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:24.860
like the Congo, but also
here in the United States,

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:27.550
including at our colleges
and universities.

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:30.960
An estimated one in five women
has been sexually assaulted

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:33.060
during her college years.

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:35.660
Of those assaults only 12% are reported

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:37.350
and of those reported assaults

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:40.560
only a fraction of the
offender's are punished.

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:41.880
That is why President Obama

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:43.420
established the White House Task Force

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:46.000
to protect students from sexual assaults.

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:48.000
In April the task force released a report

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:50.440
with recommended steps
that schools can take

00:28:50.440 --> 00:28:53.930
to hold offenders accountable
and support survivors.

00:28:53.930 --> 00:28:56.730
This struggle needs to be
waged not only by women

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:58.540
but also by men.

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:00.160
That's one of the messages behind

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:04.380
the President's and Vice
President's It's On Us campaign,

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:05.580
launched recently to stop

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:08.320
sexual assault on college campuses.

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:11.290
As a proud Yale graduate I'm
often amazed by the leadership

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:14.320
and the service of Yale
students and graduates,

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:16.310
but rarely am i as
impressed as I have been

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:18.880
with how students here have
taken up this challenge,

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:21.100
moving far beyond making a pledge,

00:29:21.100 --> 00:29:24.280
to bringing together
leaders from across campus.

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:25.950
From Yale's College Council

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.010
to communication and consent educators,

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:30.110
from the Yale Women's
Center, to sports teams,

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:32.740
and acapella groups, to build a campaign

00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:35.290
that makes everyone on this campus ask,

00:29:35.290 --> 00:29:36.810
how can we change our behavior

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:39.950
and our community to
live up to the pledge?

00:29:39.950 --> 00:29:41.570
How can we work with the administration

00:29:41.570 --> 00:29:45.220
to bring about stronger
enforcement and accountability?

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:48.080
How do we make this part of our Yale?

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:51.600
That's exactly what
It's On Us is all about.

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:54.970
At the same time, far from US campuses,

00:29:54.970 --> 00:29:58.120
we have to make UN Peacekeepers
better at protecting

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:00.300
women and girls from sexual violence.

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:03.160
One way to do that is by
improving early warning systems

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:05.290
for sexual violence and other atrocities

00:30:05.290 --> 00:30:08.930
and proactively pursuing repeat offenders.

00:30:08.930 --> 00:30:11.610
Peacekeepers in the Congo
have set up alert networks

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:13.670
that allow communities to report threats

00:30:13.670 --> 00:30:17.120
and attacks in real time
directly to UN peacekeeping bases

00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:18.650
so they can intervene

00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:21.810
and the UN created a special
unit in its Congo mission,

00:30:21.810 --> 00:30:23.560
the Force Intervention Brigade,

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:25.850
which has made real headway
disarming and defeating

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:27.550
the armed groups responsible

00:30:27.550 --> 00:30:30.900
for some of the worst
atrocities, including mass rape.

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:34.810
Yet the sad fact is that even
when peacekeepers are warned

00:30:34.810 --> 00:30:36.830
they often fail to act.

00:30:36.830 --> 00:30:39.970
A March report by the UN's
Internal Oversight Office

00:30:39.970 --> 00:30:43.420
found that in 507
attacks against civilians

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:47.840
from 2000 to 2013 peacekeepers
virtually never use force

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:49.510
to protect civilians under attack.

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:52.150
This is totally unacceptable.

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:53.870
To address the problem the United States

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:56.330
is working to ensure peacekeepers
have both the capacity

00:30:56.330 --> 00:30:59.950
and the will to protect
civilians under attack.

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:01.670
In August President Obama announced

00:31:01.670 --> 00:31:04.080
the African Peacekeeping
Rapid Response Partnership

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:07.260
or A-PREP, which will
invest 110 million dollars

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:09.480
each year for the next three to five years

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:12.040
to build the capabilities
of six countries,

00:31:12.040 --> 00:31:16.360
Ethiopia, Ghana, Rwanda,
Senegal, Tanzania, and Uganda,

00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:19.850
which have embraced a more
robust approach to peacekeeping

00:31:19.850 --> 00:31:22.020
and we are building a global consensus

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:23.680
among all peacekeeping countries,

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:25.840
including the most skeptical ones,

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:28.570
about the critical importance
of actually implementing

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:30.490
civilian protection mandates,

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:34.570
including by protecting women and girls.

00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:36.280
That is what the short game looks like

00:31:36.280 --> 00:31:38.440
in stopping sexual violence
against women and girls,

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:41.350
but of course if we want
to move beyond protecting

00:31:41.350 --> 00:31:42.760
women and girls from abuses,

00:31:42.760 --> 00:31:45.630
to empowering them to
realize their full potential

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:47.490
we need to do much more.

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:49.410
It will come as no surprise to you

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:52.210
that education is the
linchpin of our long game

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:55.190
in women's and girl's empowerment.

00:31:55.190 --> 00:31:57.560
We know that education
is a smart investment,

00:31:57.560 --> 00:31:59.550
not only in the girl's future,

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:01.400
but also in the future of their nations,

00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:04.170
as countries that do a
better job of educating girls

00:32:04.170 --> 00:32:07.000
are on average more prosperous, healthier,

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:09.200
more democratic, and more peaceful.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:10.990
That is not a coincidence.

00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:13.050
Yet while it is all well and good to say,

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:15.430
we need to ensure girls'
right to an education

00:32:15.430 --> 00:32:17.180
and educating girls is one of the

00:32:17.180 --> 00:32:19.890
smartest investment societies can make,

00:32:19.890 --> 00:32:23.060
getting girls into the
classroom and keeping them there

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:25.620
through secondary school takes much more

00:32:25.620 --> 00:32:28.850
than making school compulsory
or building classrooms.

00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:31.900
Just look at the numbers
62 million girls worldwide

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:33.400
are not in school,

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:36.550
more than 30 million of
them in sub-saharan Africa

00:32:36.550 --> 00:32:38.850
where there is so much promise.

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:42.240
To tackle this problem we need
to change the mental models,

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:44.600
attitudes, and behaviors that lie beneath

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:46.870
this persistent imbalance.

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:49.990
For instance in Tanzania
four out of every ten girls

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:52.480
are married before their 18th birthday.

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:54.200
That's because according
to a recent report

00:32:54.200 --> 00:32:57.530
by Human Rights Watch, many
communities across the country

00:32:57.530 --> 00:33:00.720
see marriage as a way to protect
girls from premarital sex

00:33:00.720 --> 00:33:02.880
and pregnancy, both of which are seen

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:05.400
as undermining a family's honor.

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:07.020
In addition as we know brides' families

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:08.980
receive doweries from grooms' families

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:10.420
giving them a strong incentive

00:33:10.420 --> 00:33:12.610
to marry their daughters early.

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:14.520
As a result girls are married off young,

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:16.180
forcing many to leave school

00:33:16.180 --> 00:33:18.370
and even those who stay
in school after marriage

00:33:18.370 --> 00:33:20.430
are often pressured by
school administrators

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:23.930
and family members to drop
out when they become pregnant.

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:26.140
Girls who marry early are more vulnerable

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:27.960
to a range of other abuses,

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:31.410
including domestic
violence and marital rape.

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:34.950
It is impossible to ensure
more girls in Tanzania

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:38.320
find their seats in the
classroom and hold on to them

00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:41.930
without these societies
tackling the underlying beliefs

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:45.560
and practices that are
driving girls from school.

00:33:45.560 --> 00:33:47.680
That means changing
attitudes and communities

00:33:47.680 --> 00:33:49.470
about child marriage and dowries,

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:51.960
and convincing educators to find ways

00:33:51.960 --> 00:33:55.130
to allow pregnant girls
to continue their studies.

00:33:55.130 --> 00:33:58.190
Behind every one of the
obstacles to girls' education

00:33:58.190 --> 00:34:01.370
is a fear or prejudice
that we must address.

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:05.160
It is fear of empowered women
that drives extremist groups

00:34:05.160 --> 00:34:07.180
to abduct girls from their classrooms

00:34:07.180 --> 00:34:10.360
as they did with the Chibok
schoolgirls in Nigeria,

00:34:10.360 --> 00:34:12.380
and fear that drove them to try to kill

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:17.380
this year's remarkable Nobel
Prize winner, Malala Yousafzai,

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:20.350
for daring to champion girls' right

00:34:20.350 --> 00:34:22.940
to an education in Pakistan,

00:34:22.940 --> 00:34:26.390
and it is deep-seated
prejudice that causes families

00:34:26.390 --> 00:34:28.320
to see educating their daughters

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:31.630
as less important than
educating their sons.

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:34.630
The only way to overcome
these obstacles to empowerment

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:37.000
is to challenge the dangerous idea

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:40.520
that a girl's education is
a privilege or even a threat

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:43.860
rather than what we know
it to be, which is a right.

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:46.870
In some instances changes
in the belief systems

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:48.350
underlying these practices can be

00:34:48.350 --> 00:34:50.410
fostered by public policies.

00:34:50.410 --> 00:34:51.910
In West Bengal, for example,

00:34:51.910 --> 00:34:54.270
a political affirmative action program

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:57.730
put women leaders in village
government for the first time.

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:00.700
Within seven years men's individual bias

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:02.510
against the capacity of women leaders

00:35:02.510 --> 00:35:04.530
almost fully disappeared.

00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:06.820
Seeing women leaders in
positions of authority

00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:10.330
also gave parents higher
aspirations for their daughters

00:35:10.330 --> 00:35:13.980
and raised girls
expectations for themselves,

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:17.450
and we know full well
that the developed world

00:35:17.450 --> 00:35:20.070
needs public policy oriented around

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:23.100
girls' and women's empowerment, as well.

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:25.177
Last week Germany passed legislation

00:35:25.177 --> 00:35:27.860
requiring the country's leading companies

00:35:27.860 --> 00:35:30.270
to have at least 30% of the membership

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:33.150
on their executive
boards be held by women,

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:36.140
up from the current level
of approximately 7%.

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:39.330
This in a country led
by a female Chancellor

00:35:39.330 --> 00:35:42.600
with a Parliament that is
approximately 40% female,

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:46.690
which is more than double the
proportion in the US Congress.

00:35:46.690 --> 00:35:49.000
When we invest in
empowering women and girls,

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:50.970
the results come full-circle.

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:52.640
Women and girls who are better educated

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:55.230
can demand more accountable
justice systems,

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:56.430
which are more effective at

00:35:56.430 --> 00:35:59.110
prosecuting cases of sexual violence.

00:35:59.110 --> 00:36:01.290
They will also be better
informed about their rights

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:03.390
and thus be better equipped to defend them

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:06.630
and to demand redress
when they are violated.

00:36:06.630 --> 00:36:09.540
It makes her a virtuous
positive feedback loop

00:36:09.540 --> 00:36:11.670
by investing in empowering women and girls

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:14.510
we help create societies
where the sexual violence

00:36:14.510 --> 00:36:16.910
against them is less likely to occur.

00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:19.440
By playing the long game reduce the need

00:36:19.440 --> 00:36:20.893
to play a short game.

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:23.650
Now I've spoken to you
about the importance

00:36:23.650 --> 00:36:27.080
of looking beyond stopping
the current Ebola outbreak

00:36:27.080 --> 00:36:30.300
to how we can prevent similar
outbreaks in the future

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:32.750
and I've spoken about
how in addition to taking

00:36:32.750 --> 00:36:35.220
immediate steps to end sexual violence,

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:37.250
we also need to invest more fully

00:36:37.250 --> 00:36:39.870
in women's and girls' empowerment.

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:43.850
In both instances the threat
in front of us is so urgent

00:36:43.850 --> 00:36:47.090
and the suffering it
causes is so devastating

00:36:47.090 --> 00:36:51.500
that it can be easy to lose
sight of long-term investments.

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:53.990
This problem of being
consumed by responding

00:36:53.990 --> 00:36:57.810
to the here and now of a
crisis is especially acute

00:36:57.810 --> 00:37:00.660
with regard to the final
threat that I want to discuss,

00:37:00.660 --> 00:37:02.460
violent extremism.

00:37:02.460 --> 00:37:04.170
The violence and suffering caused

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:05.930
by violent extremist groups

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:08.980
calls out for our immediate response,

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:10.600
making it particularly challenging

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:13.770
to maintain a focus on the long term.

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:15.390
In a time when we wonder if we have

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.880
lost our capacity to feel horror,

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:21.370
the barbarism of these groups
continues to sicken us.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:24.140
In its vicious campaign
against the Yazidi people,

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:26.870
ISIL continues to abduct women and girls,

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:29.030
hold them captive, and
rape them systematically

00:37:29.030 --> 00:37:32.910
before auctioning them off
like cattle at markets.

00:37:32.910 --> 00:37:35.430
Last week in northern Nigeria
in a series of attacks

00:37:35.430 --> 00:37:37.742
bearing the hallmarks of Boko Haram,

00:37:37.742 --> 00:37:40.150
45 people were killed
by two suicide bombers

00:37:40.150 --> 00:37:41.370
in a crowded marketplace

00:37:41.370 --> 00:37:44.340
and at least 120 people
were killed by bombs

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:47.640
and gunmen in an attack on a major mosque.

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:49.210
It goes without saying that

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:51.550
because terrorist groups like ISIL

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:53.080
pose a threat to Americans,

00:37:53.080 --> 00:37:55.100
and to international peace and security

00:37:55.100 --> 00:37:58.510
we must rally other
nations to confront them.

00:37:58.510 --> 00:38:00.440
This is why in May the President launched

00:38:00.440 --> 00:38:03.120
the Counterterrorism
Partnerships Fund at West Point

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:04.860
to build the capacity of our partners

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:07.270
on the frontlines in this shared effort

00:38:07.270 --> 00:38:10.010
and why the United States
has rallied over 60 partners

00:38:10.010 --> 00:38:13.940
to a global coalition to
degrade and defeat ISIL.

00:38:13.940 --> 00:38:16.210
Many of these partners
are already contributing

00:38:16.210 --> 00:38:18.870
in concrete ways, whether
through military support

00:38:18.870 --> 00:38:21.190
or through addressing the
humanitarian catastrophes

00:38:21.190 --> 00:38:24.080
that ISIL's terror has helped create.

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:26.250
Another critical part of
responding to this threat

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:28.810
is stopping the flow of foreign fighters

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:32.120
who increasingly fill the ranks
of these extremist groups.

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:34.010
Suicide bombers not only from Fallujah

00:38:34.010 --> 00:38:36.120
but also from Florida,

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:39.100
fighters not only from
Peshawar but from Paris.

00:38:39.100 --> 00:38:42.730
It is estimated that more
than 16,000 foreign fighters

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:45.550
from more than 90 countries
have traveled to Syria

00:38:45.550 --> 00:38:48.800
since the conflict began, 16,000 people,

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:50.530
and this includes many people who hold

00:38:50.530 --> 00:38:53.060
American and EU passports.

00:38:53.060 --> 00:38:54.590
President Obama has taken the lead

00:38:54.590 --> 00:38:56.010
in addressing the threat posed

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:57.690
by foreign terrorist fighters.

00:38:57.690 --> 00:39:00.230
In September he chaired a
UN Security Council summit

00:39:00.230 --> 00:39:02.220
at the UN on this problem,

00:39:02.220 --> 00:39:05.360
in which Member States committed
to taking decisive steps

00:39:05.360 --> 00:39:07.460
including stopping the flow of funding

00:39:07.460 --> 00:39:10.400
and enacting national laws
to make it more difficult

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:13.400
for these fighters to join such conflicts.

00:39:13.400 --> 00:39:15.250
But if we do not play the long game

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:18.150
in addressing the hatred
fueling these groups

00:39:18.150 --> 00:39:21.010
and the reasons individuals
are drawn to their ideology,

00:39:21.010 --> 00:39:23.750
the waves of extremists will keep coming.

00:39:23.750 --> 00:39:26.710
Here Syria offers an important lesson

00:39:26.710 --> 00:39:28.400
because when we look
at the Syrian conflict

00:39:28.400 --> 00:39:30.750
we are reminded that ISIL's barbarism

00:39:30.750 --> 00:39:33.860
has been fueled by the
barbarism of the Assad regime.

00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:37.130
A regime whose ongoing
atrocities continue to be

00:39:37.130 --> 00:39:40.000
ISIL's most fertile recruiting tool.

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:43.330
A regime that during Ramadan
calibrated its bombing runs

00:39:43.330 --> 00:39:45.960
to coincide with the times and places

00:39:45.960 --> 00:39:49.000
where Muslims were
meeting to break the fast.

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:50.700
A regime that dropped chemical weapons

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:53.080
and barrel bombs on unarmed civilians,

00:39:53.080 --> 00:39:54.490
a regime that is responsible for

00:39:54.490 --> 00:39:56.520
torturing thousands of detainees,

00:39:56.520 --> 00:40:00.220
and documenting their torment
in harrowing photographs,

00:40:00.220 --> 00:40:02.683
whose victims were given serial numbers,

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:06.110
and their torture, starvation,

00:40:06.110 --> 00:40:09.840
and killings meticulously documented.

00:40:09.840 --> 00:40:13.010
So those who argue that we
must work with the Assad regime

00:40:13.010 --> 00:40:16.080
to vanquish the threat
of ISIL are wrongheaded

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:19.540
about this being productive
for American interests.

00:40:19.540 --> 00:40:21.250
Not only would partnering with Assad

00:40:21.250 --> 00:40:24.350
go against what America
stands for and believes in,

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:27.630
it would be a huge boon for
extremism in the region,

00:40:27.630 --> 00:40:28.840
and the world.

00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:32.160
In Syria the enemy of our enemy is not

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:33.993
and will never be our friend.

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:36.870
The lesson to be drawn
from the Syrian conflict

00:40:36.870 --> 00:40:39.110
is one that can be applied
far beyond its borders,

00:40:39.110 --> 00:40:42.150
extremism thrives in places
where people have no hope,

00:40:42.150 --> 00:40:43.860
where brutality is systematized,

00:40:43.860 --> 00:40:45.910
where impunity is emdemic.

00:40:45.910 --> 00:40:49.410
Extremism grows in dark
places where there is no hope

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:52.320
or light to let other ambitions grow.

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:54.510
Tackling violent extremism also requires

00:40:54.510 --> 00:40:56.910
lifting up the voices of Muslims

00:40:56.910 --> 00:40:59.410
and people of all faiths
who denounce terrorist acts

00:40:59.410 --> 00:41:01.510
committed in the name of Islam.

00:41:01.510 --> 00:41:05.150
In September, 126 Muslim
scholars sent a letter

00:41:05.150 --> 00:41:07.530
to the head of ISIL saying,

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:10.380
it is forbidden in Islam
to kill the innocent.

00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:13.730
It is forbidden in Islam to
kill emissaries, ambassadors,

00:41:13.730 --> 00:41:16.230
and diplomats, hence
it is forbidden to kill

00:41:16.230 --> 00:41:18.320
journalists and aid workers.

00:41:18.320 --> 00:41:21.890
It is forbidden in Islam to
harm or mistreat in any way

00:41:21.890 --> 00:41:25.690
Christians or any people of the scripture.

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:28.090
It is obligatory to consider the Yazidis

00:41:28.090 --> 00:41:30.120
as people of the scripture.

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:34.203
The reintroduction of slavery
is forbidden in Islam.

00:41:35.290 --> 00:41:38.010
Now I didn't hear about
this letter when it came out

00:41:38.010 --> 00:41:40.020
and chances are neither did you,

00:41:40.020 --> 00:41:41.830
and neither did so many people,

00:41:41.830 --> 00:41:43.500
particularly in the Muslim world,

00:41:43.500 --> 00:41:46.750
to whom the letter is also directed.

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:48.840
I first heard about it a few weeks ago

00:41:48.840 --> 00:41:50.810
when the UN High Commissioner
for Human Rights,

00:41:50.810 --> 00:41:53.460
Zeid Ra'ad al-Hussein, also a Muslim,

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:55.360
read it before the Security Council.

00:41:55.360 --> 00:41:58.957
He also told the Council
of ISIL's attacks, quote,

00:41:58.957 --> 00:42:00.920
"The authors of these crimes are people

00:42:00.920 --> 00:42:03.320
whose actions are nothing but disgusting

00:42:03.320 --> 00:42:06.120
and whose consciences have demonstrably

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:08.430
been annihilated," end quote.

00:42:08.430 --> 00:42:10.390
More messages like the cleric's

00:42:10.390 --> 00:42:13.343
and like the High
Commissioner's need to be heard.

00:42:14.200 --> 00:42:16.840
In addition to lifting
up the voices of Muslims

00:42:16.840 --> 00:42:19.830
who denounce terrorism,
we also have to close

00:42:19.830 --> 00:42:22.910
the detention facility at Guantanamo Bay.

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:24.240
The facility should never have been

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:27.600
opened in the first place and
continues to make us less safe

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:30.710
and to undermine our
standing in the world.

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:33.500
Years ago the Supreme
Court rejected the premise

00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:35.810
for opening GTMO, which was that detainees

00:42:35.810 --> 00:42:38.490
would not be able to
challenge their detention.

00:42:38.490 --> 00:42:41.480
The vast majority of detainees
once held at Guantanamo

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:44.390
have been transferred, including 530

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:47.230
transferred under the
previous administration.

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:50.420
As a US diplomat I
can't tell you how often

00:42:50.420 --> 00:42:52.660
other governments point to GTMO

00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:56.930
as a way of defending
their repressive practices

00:42:56.930 --> 00:43:00.570
or how much its sustained
existence continues to weigh

00:43:00.570 --> 00:43:03.840
on our relationships with
close partners and allies,

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:06.610
and extremist groups
continue to use Guantanamo

00:43:06.610 --> 00:43:09.690
as a propaganda tool to recruit those

00:43:09.690 --> 00:43:11.290
who seek to do us harm

00:43:11.290 --> 00:43:15.490
and as a justification for
their abhorrent violence.

00:43:15.490 --> 00:43:16.740
President Obama has made clear

00:43:16.740 --> 00:43:18.790
his desire to close Guantanamo,

00:43:18.790 --> 00:43:21.630
but since 2009 Congress
has imposed restrictions

00:43:21.630 --> 00:43:24.170
that have constrained
our ability to do so,

00:43:24.170 --> 00:43:26.670
including placing limits on our ability

00:43:26.670 --> 00:43:28.490
to transfer detainees to foreign countries

00:43:28.490 --> 00:43:30.350
and imposing a prohibition

00:43:30.350 --> 00:43:33.500
on transferring detainees
to the United States,

00:43:33.500 --> 00:43:35.220
even those we have the capacity

00:43:35.220 --> 00:43:38.560
to effectively prosecute for their crimes.

00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:40.910
Another argument should not be necessary

00:43:40.910 --> 00:43:42.730
but please listen to this one.

00:43:42.730 --> 00:43:45.860
The United States is spending
nearly half a billion dollars

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:48.750
annually to operate
the detention facility,

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:52.070
which currently holds 142 detainees.

00:43:52.070 --> 00:43:55.580
That works out to roughly three
million dollars per detainee

00:43:57.486 --> 00:44:00.200
per year or approximately thirty times

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:01.760
what it costs to hold a prisoner

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:04.450
in a federal supermax prison.

00:44:04.450 --> 00:44:06.110
The kind of facility from which

00:44:06.110 --> 00:44:09.590
no prisoner has ever escaped.

00:44:09.590 --> 00:44:11.960
That same half a billion
dollars could be spent

00:44:11.960 --> 00:44:15.310
training nurses to stop the
spread of Ebola in West Africa

00:44:15.310 --> 00:44:18.620
or investing in girls' education
in Nigeria or Afghanistan,

00:44:18.620 --> 00:44:20.660
or strengthening aviation security

00:44:20.660 --> 00:44:22.400
and information-sharing tools

00:44:22.400 --> 00:44:24.870
to track down foreign terrorist fighters,

00:44:24.870 --> 00:44:26.920
or any of the other long-term investments,

00:44:26.920 --> 00:44:30.963
domestic or international, that
too often gets short shrift.

00:44:32.030 --> 00:44:35.390
Now at times it can feel
as though the United States

00:44:35.390 --> 00:44:37.330
is shouldering an unfair burden

00:44:37.330 --> 00:44:38.730
in responding to global threats

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:40.720
and that the American
people are the only ones

00:44:40.720 --> 00:44:43.960
being asked to make sacrifices to try

00:44:43.960 --> 00:44:46.100
to ensure a more secure and stable world,

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:47.260
but we are not.

00:44:47.260 --> 00:44:49.150
We live in a world full of people

00:44:49.150 --> 00:44:51.040
willing to put everything on the line

00:44:51.040 --> 00:44:53.760
for the future of their
families, their nations,

00:44:53.760 --> 00:44:57.050
and by extension for
the world that we share.

00:44:57.050 --> 00:44:59.140
That dedication is what drove so many

00:44:59.140 --> 00:45:01.560
of the young Liberians,
Guineans, and Sierra Leoneans

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:02.860
that I met on my recent trip.

00:45:02.860 --> 00:45:04.430
People risking their lives,

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:07.150
as well as alienation
from their communities

00:45:07.150 --> 00:45:10.040
by volunteering to serve in
the Ebola treatment centers

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:11.870
and in the burial teams.

00:45:11.870 --> 00:45:14.230
As one young volunteer
told me in Freetown,

00:45:14.230 --> 00:45:16.087
the capital of Sierra Leone, quote,

00:45:16.087 --> 00:45:18.350
"If we leave our brothers
and sisters to die

00:45:18.350 --> 00:45:20.580
who knows, it might be us next.

00:45:20.580 --> 00:45:23.630
It is a point of duty," end quote.

00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:25.610
It is what continues to drive schoolgirls

00:45:25.610 --> 00:45:27.950
in northern Nigeria to
show up to class every day,

00:45:27.950 --> 00:45:30.110
girls who are not willing to let the fear

00:45:30.110 --> 00:45:32.920
of seeing other girls
kidnapped by extremists

00:45:32.920 --> 00:45:36.070
keep them from claiming
their right to an education,

00:45:36.070 --> 00:45:37.740
and it is what drove the fearless

00:45:37.740 --> 00:45:41.030
Libyan human rights
defender, Sawa Bugaighis,

00:45:41.030 --> 00:45:42.670
to stand up for the rights of her people

00:45:42.670 --> 00:45:44.230
despite constant death threats,

00:45:44.230 --> 00:45:48.450
first under the Gaddafi regime
and later from extremists.

00:45:48.450 --> 00:45:52.120
On June 25th, 2014 Sawa
urged her fellow Libyans

00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:54.270
not to be intimidated by extremists

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:55.290
who had threatened to attack

00:45:55.290 --> 00:45:58.350
anyone who voted in
parliamentary elections.

00:45:58.350 --> 00:46:00.900
She even posted photos of
herself casting her ballot

00:46:00.900 --> 00:46:04.230
on her Facebook page to show
that she would not be cowed

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:06.840
by the death threats she
had received or by fear.

00:46:06.840 --> 00:46:08.140
She was killed that night.

00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:11.807
All of these people, the
Jacksons, and the Sawas,

00:46:11.807 --> 00:46:13.077
and the Nigerian school girls,

00:46:13.077 --> 00:46:17.270
and so many others are our
partners in the long game,

00:46:17.270 --> 00:46:18.820
and they have high expectations,

00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:20.940
not just for themselves but for us.

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:22.840
They believe that the
United States will live up

00:46:22.840 --> 00:46:25.380
to our highest principles
by standing with them.

00:46:25.380 --> 00:46:27.350
They believe that we will prove worthy

00:46:27.350 --> 00:46:29.390
of wielding the tremendous responsibility

00:46:29.390 --> 00:46:32.420
that comes with our tremendous power.

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:34.230
That we will not only play the long game

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:37.090
because it is in our
self-interest, which it is,

00:46:37.090 --> 00:46:39.550
but because we believe
it is right to advance

00:46:39.550 --> 00:46:43.633
the dignity of people around
the world, which it is.

00:46:45.210 --> 00:46:48.980
It is and must be a constant
struggle for all of us

00:46:48.980 --> 00:46:51.040
to live up to these expectations

00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:52.803
and to live up to these principles.

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:57.900
In the United States and
abroad people recognize

00:46:57.900 --> 00:47:00.300
that this is a struggle
that the American people

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:01.850
have embraced and that we who have

00:47:01.850 --> 00:47:05.570
the privilege of representing
them must embrace as well.

00:47:05.570 --> 00:47:09.410
It is the constant struggle
that makes us who we are.

00:47:09.410 --> 00:47:13.120
It is why our partners and the
world expects so much of us

00:47:13.120 --> 00:47:16.030
and that is not a burden
that weighs us down,

00:47:16.030 --> 00:47:18.320
it is a wind that lifts us up.

00:47:18.320 --> 00:47:19.415
Thank you.

00:47:19.415 --> 00:47:22.582
(audience applauding)

00:47:31.539 --> 00:47:32.372
Thank you.

00:47:43.850 --> 00:47:45.550
- Thank you, very much.

00:47:45.550 --> 00:47:46.410
I'm Jeff Brenzel

00:47:46.410 --> 00:47:48.420
I'm the master of Timothy Dwight College

00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:51.390
and the Steward of the Chubb Fellowship,

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:52.320
and knowing the stress

00:47:52.320 --> 00:47:55.410
and strain of Ambassador Power's work,

00:47:55.410 --> 00:47:58.280
not to mention the
relentless sense of urgency

00:47:58.280 --> 00:48:00.163
that rises up to meet her every day,

00:48:01.060 --> 00:48:03.010
I'm both impressed and
grateful that she came today

00:48:03.010 --> 00:48:05.730
to participate in a class

00:48:05.730 --> 00:48:09.120
here with Paul Gewirtz, Jake
Sullivan, and Harold Koh,

00:48:09.120 --> 00:48:13.000
has stayed to give this University
Chubb Fellowship address

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:16.980
and we'll have dinner later
with 120 undergraduates.

00:48:16.980 --> 00:48:17.817
- Don't forget the reception.

00:48:17.817 --> 00:48:18.964
- And the reception.

00:48:18.964 --> 00:48:20.090
(audience laughing)

00:48:20.090 --> 00:48:23.950
- I like what she said
then about the long game.

00:48:23.950 --> 00:48:26.530
Hendon Chubb and the
many prior Chubb fellows

00:48:26.530 --> 00:48:28.620
would applaud her investment here today

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:32.330
in you and in us.

00:48:32.330 --> 00:48:35.990
I hope she will see a
return on the investment,

00:48:35.990 --> 00:48:40.070
both from those inside and
those outside this room.

00:48:40.070 --> 00:48:42.250
We're privileged to have her here,

00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:44.650
we're privileged in general,

00:48:44.650 --> 00:48:48.400
and that privilege brings its obligations.

00:48:48.400 --> 00:48:52.580
Let's hope that we can
seize our opportunities

00:48:52.580 --> 00:48:56.100
in the ways that she has seized hers.

00:48:56.100 --> 00:48:58.143
So let's have a few questions.

00:48:59.020 --> 00:49:01.710
You see two microphones here in the aisle.

00:49:01.710 --> 00:49:02.880
If you have a question

00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:06.360
please make your way to
those microphones now.

00:49:06.360 --> 00:49:10.310
Some of you are positioned
advantageously for this

00:49:11.330 --> 00:49:16.020
and because time is
short please do your best

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:21.020
to speak briefly and ask a clear question.

00:49:21.590 --> 00:49:23.300
Okay, thanks very much

00:49:23.300 --> 00:49:26.400
and Nicole because you're a TD student,

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:27.450
we'll start with you.

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:31.280
- [Nicole] Thank you very much Miss Power.

00:49:31.280 --> 00:49:34.870
A recent profile of you
in the Vogue Magazine

00:49:34.870 --> 00:49:38.280
has your friend and a former
colleague of yours saying,

00:49:38.280 --> 00:49:40.570
people with Samantha's
moral compass and compassion

00:49:40.570 --> 00:49:43.560
don't usually make it
to such ranks of power.

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:45.320
My question follows on from that.

00:49:45.320 --> 00:49:48.840
I've always respected your
ability to criticize governments

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:53.670
and other bodies, both
abroad and at home in the US,

00:49:53.670 --> 00:49:56.540
but your position now as a
representative for the US

00:49:56.540 --> 00:49:59.460
requires I assume that
you defend and promote

00:50:00.361 --> 00:50:04.530
the position of the US even
when it might be against

00:50:04.530 --> 00:50:07.290
or different to your
own personal position,

00:50:07.290 --> 00:50:09.550
or even when it is criticized by other

00:50:09.550 --> 00:50:11.223
human rights organizations.

00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:13.530
I wanted to know if you could please speak

00:50:13.530 --> 00:50:15.370
to the challenges holding such a position

00:50:15.370 --> 00:50:17.690
with such constraints versus say,

00:50:17.690 --> 00:50:19.290
being an independent journalist.

00:50:21.680 --> 00:50:23.340
- Great, thank you.

00:50:23.340 --> 00:50:24.740
- Easy one.
- Yeah.

00:50:24.740 --> 00:50:27.440
I was hoping when when the
question started by quoting Vogue

00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:29.951
I was hoping we were trending
in a good direction but.

00:50:29.951 --> 00:50:32.960
(audience laughing)

00:50:32.960 --> 00:50:36.280
Let me just, I think the
best way to answer it is

00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:42.520
just to describe the
blessing that I feel everyday

00:50:42.520 --> 00:50:45.223
being particularly in
the job that I'm in now,

00:50:46.064 --> 00:50:48.000
getting to represent the United States,

00:50:48.000 --> 00:50:49.700
I'm an Irish immigrant to this country,

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:51.300
getting to sit in front of the placard,

00:50:51.300 --> 00:50:56.300
having the weight of our
diplomatic resources,

00:50:56.540 --> 00:51:01.540
and our means and our soft
power behind me everyday,

00:51:02.640 --> 00:51:06.370
on behalf of the same causes
I champion my entire life,

00:51:06.370 --> 00:51:10.670
from you know the basement
of Beinecke Library

00:51:10.670 --> 00:51:13.720
or you know wherever I was
working on a given day.

00:51:13.720 --> 00:51:18.720
I mean, in the old days when
I wrote a column for instance

00:51:19.150 --> 00:51:21.760
I used to have a column at Time Magazine

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:24.930
or if I wrote a long New Yorker piece,

00:51:24.930 --> 00:51:26.870
my hope was that someone at a

00:51:26.870 --> 00:51:28.670
much lower level than mine now

00:51:28.670 --> 00:51:31.350
would read that and would
bring it into the meetings

00:51:31.350 --> 00:51:35.700
and try to inject a
perspective from the field,

00:51:35.700 --> 00:51:37.920
from, you know, from people
who could speak to the ways

00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:41.600
in which policies were
affecting real people

00:51:41.600 --> 00:51:44.030
far from, as it were, the boardroom

00:51:44.030 --> 00:51:45.920
or far from the bureaucracy.

00:51:45.920 --> 00:51:49.820
So I just feel very incredibly privileged

00:51:50.860 --> 00:51:53.450
to be able to take that perspective

00:51:53.450 --> 00:51:56.030
and I just give you a couple examples

00:51:56.030 --> 00:52:01.030
of how I think bridging
those worlds can be

00:52:01.050 --> 00:52:03.283
really powerful and important.

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:07.210
One I mentioned already, Jackson Naimah,

00:52:07.210 --> 00:52:10.230
the Security Council
occasionally brings in people

00:52:10.230 --> 00:52:14.390
from outside UN officialdom

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:17.020
but relatively rarely still

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:19.390
and when we did this meeting for

00:52:19.390 --> 00:52:21.610
the first, again, emergency meeting

00:52:21.610 --> 00:52:24.750
on a public health crisis in
the 70 year history of the UN.

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:26.240
There'd been a really important

00:52:26.240 --> 00:52:29.240
AIDS Security Council meeting before

00:52:29.240 --> 00:52:31.870
but to think how you puncture

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:34.930
what people are bringing,
the preexisting alibis,

00:52:34.930 --> 00:52:38.830
and biases, and
indifference, or even concern

00:52:38.830 --> 00:52:41.230
but misinformation or whatever that people

00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:43.630
are bringing to this meeting,

00:52:43.630 --> 00:52:48.250
and to find a Liberian health
worker who actually was

00:52:48.250 --> 00:52:50.590
the person at the gates
having to turn people away

00:52:50.590 --> 00:52:51.980
'cause there just weren't enough beds,

00:52:51.980 --> 00:52:53.590
and for him to describe what it was like

00:52:53.590 --> 00:52:56.990
to tell a father that no he
could not deposit his child

00:52:56.990 --> 00:53:00.110
in the Council, if I have the
chance as the UN Ambassador

00:53:00.110 --> 00:53:04.100
to bring Jackson's voice
into UN Security Council

00:53:04.100 --> 00:53:07.760
deliberations which are usually, you know,

00:53:07.760 --> 00:53:12.073
a little more robotic, I mean,

00:53:13.050 --> 00:53:16.170
and if the price is simply that having

00:53:16.170 --> 00:53:19.710
argued for something
internally and not succeeded

00:53:19.710 --> 00:53:23.500
that I you know get to go
out and still defend the US,

00:53:23.500 --> 00:53:25.520
I mean that's a very modest price to pay.

00:53:25.520 --> 00:53:29.270
Another example is we pushed
the Obama Administration,

00:53:29.270 --> 00:53:32.870
pushed hard to try to
get the crimes in Syria

00:53:32.870 --> 00:53:35.350
referred to the
International Criminal Court

00:53:35.350 --> 00:53:38.970
and Russia cast its fourth veto

00:53:38.970 --> 00:53:41.690
in the Syria context over that issue

00:53:41.690 --> 00:53:45.150
and you know, when I was
sitting down with my team

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:49.200
to think about how can we
capture the stakes of this

00:53:49.200 --> 00:53:51.320
and this is an not a story of success

00:53:51.320 --> 00:53:52.540
from a policy standpoint,

00:53:52.540 --> 00:53:54.520
but nonetheless, there
are all of these people

00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:56.420
out there in Syria
who've been barrel bombed

00:53:56.420 --> 00:54:01.166
by this regime, bee tortured,
have been gassed with sarin,

00:54:01.166 --> 00:54:04.700
and now with this veto
that's coming tomorrow,

00:54:04.700 --> 00:54:08.620
the Russians will prevent
those crimes from ever being

00:54:08.620 --> 00:54:11.850
or for the time being
anyway being adjudicated

00:54:11.850 --> 00:54:13.600
or prosecuted in court,

00:54:13.600 --> 00:54:17.870
so we tracked down a
chemical weapons survivor

00:54:17.870 --> 00:54:19.280
and I was able to say to the council

00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:21.560
and to my Russian colleague
and Chinese colleague,

00:54:21.560 --> 00:54:25.900
who also vetoed, you know, unfortunately

00:54:25.900 --> 00:54:29.130
because of these vetoes today the ICC

00:54:29.130 --> 00:54:31.400
will not be able to hear the testimony

00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:34.683
of people who survived the
chemical weapons attacks.

00:54:35.714 --> 00:54:38.230
It would be left to a
court normally to decide

00:54:38.230 --> 00:54:40.470
what is true and what is false,

00:54:40.470 --> 00:54:42.440
but here is the testimony of one person

00:54:42.440 --> 00:54:44.400
who would have liked to testify,

00:54:44.400 --> 00:54:47.690
and I just, in his own
voice basically described

00:54:47.690 --> 00:54:49.100
what had happened to him

00:54:49.100 --> 00:54:51.680
and brought, we ended up
being able to bring him in

00:54:51.680 --> 00:54:53.860
to the Security Council Chamber,

00:54:53.860 --> 00:54:57.040
and he and others again it's
small compared to the veto

00:54:57.040 --> 00:54:58.050
which was so devastating

00:54:58.050 --> 00:55:02.650
but to be able to inject that
perspective into deliberations

00:55:02.650 --> 00:55:05.033
again is the privilege of a lifetime,

00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:07.720
compared to to writing a column

00:55:07.720 --> 00:55:10.220
and again hoping someone
will read my column,

00:55:10.220 --> 00:55:11.320
I'd take this any day.

00:55:13.360 --> 00:55:15.750
- [Audience Member] In
thinking about the long run

00:55:15.750 --> 00:55:19.550
is US diplomacy doing anything to separate

00:55:19.550 --> 00:55:22.740
the followers from the
leadership of the terrorist group

00:55:22.740 --> 00:55:25.623
and somehow welcoming them
back into civil society?

00:55:27.060 --> 00:55:28.140
- It's an excellent question.

00:55:28.140 --> 00:55:32.660
I mean, I think that there
are a number of programs

00:55:32.660 --> 00:55:37.067
now that exists including a
very noteworthy one in the UAE

00:55:39.140 --> 00:55:43.010
which is for people who have chosen

00:55:43.010 --> 00:55:46.750
to leave violent extremist groups

00:55:47.613 --> 00:55:52.110
and I think thinking through
not only the pressure side

00:55:52.110 --> 00:55:54.490
of the equation, namely
what are the disincentives,

00:55:54.490 --> 00:55:57.160
you know what are the
rewards that can be placed

00:55:57.160 --> 00:55:58.617
for the arrest or capture of individuals

00:55:58.617 --> 00:56:01.400
who are behind very nefarious plotting,

00:56:01.400 --> 00:56:04.480
but thinking about how to
pull away those followers,

00:56:04.480 --> 00:56:06.840
whether through social media

00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:09.050
and places that they're
very, very active to try to,

00:56:09.050 --> 00:56:12.000
right now, the terrorists in ISIL

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:16.640
have taken significant advantage
of social media platforms

00:56:16.640 --> 00:56:20.830
and it's really important
that we who are trying to

00:56:20.830 --> 00:56:22.490
contest that message,

00:56:22.490 --> 00:56:24.726
but not just we the the kinds of clerics

00:56:24.726 --> 00:56:27.730
that I described in in my remarks,

00:56:27.730 --> 00:56:29.980
that they are taking advantage
of those platforms as well,

00:56:29.980 --> 00:56:31.440
and pulling them away.

00:56:31.440 --> 00:56:33.810
So we're drawing on on everything

00:56:33.810 --> 00:56:35.090
in the Department of Homeland Security,

00:56:35.090 --> 00:56:37.900
in the Department of Justice,
across the US Government,

00:56:37.900 --> 00:56:40.380
in terms of the State Department
and our public diplomacy

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:43.400
to try to project messages

00:56:43.400 --> 00:56:44.710
of the kind that you're describing,

00:56:44.710 --> 00:56:46.970
which is that there is a place to go

00:56:47.805 --> 00:56:50.020
and that you're not, you
know, that at a certain point

00:56:50.020 --> 00:56:51.060
you're not too far gone.

00:56:51.060 --> 00:56:53.450
On the other hand if
people have been part of

00:56:53.450 --> 00:56:55.370
executing American journalists

00:56:55.370 --> 00:56:58.150
and you know, the kinds
of horrors that have been

00:56:58.150 --> 00:57:00.463
perpetrated in Syria and Iraq,

00:57:01.490 --> 00:57:04.120
you know, we also have responsibilities,

00:57:04.120 --> 00:57:07.540
particularly to American
citizens to pursue prosecution

00:57:07.540 --> 00:57:09.960
and so forth, so, you know,

00:57:09.960 --> 00:57:14.330
I think separating out people
who have been instrumental

00:57:14.330 --> 00:57:17.170
in violent acts against civilians

00:57:17.170 --> 00:57:19.740
from those who sort of thought
it might be a cool idea

00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:24.110
to go to Syria for you
know, their school break

00:57:24.110 --> 00:57:27.040
and then got stuck there, we need a way

00:57:27.040 --> 00:57:30.653
and to continue to strengthen
the ways of distinguishing.

00:57:32.660 --> 00:57:34.740
- [Audience Member]
Ambassador could you comment

00:57:34.740 --> 00:57:39.160
on the United Nations
conference on climate change

00:57:39.160 --> 00:57:41.760
that is starting this week in Lima, Peru

00:57:41.760 --> 00:57:43.990
and also give us your projection

00:57:43.990 --> 00:57:47.240
as to how the result of
that conference might be

00:57:48.232 --> 00:57:51.160
implemented by the President
and by the Congress.

00:57:51.160 --> 00:57:52.730
- Thank you.

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:57.730
Well one of the reasons
that President Obama

00:57:58.380 --> 00:58:00.870
and the Chinese Premier
made the announcement

00:58:00.870 --> 00:58:04.880
that they did, when they
did, was to try to inject

00:58:04.880 --> 00:58:09.330
into that conference momentum
out of the bilateral agreement

00:58:09.330 --> 00:58:11.640
that we reached with China

00:58:11.640 --> 00:58:16.190
and I will say now as
a somewhat of a veteran

00:58:16.190 --> 00:58:17.590
of multilateral diplomacy,

00:58:17.590 --> 00:58:20.910
having worked on it for four
plus years at the White House

00:58:20.910 --> 00:58:25.313
and now been in my current
job for more than a year,

00:58:26.940 --> 00:58:31.940
often the best ingredient for successful

00:58:32.890 --> 00:58:37.890
multilateral diplomacy is
strong bilateral agreements

00:58:39.650 --> 00:58:41.680
that then amalgamate,

00:58:41.680 --> 00:58:46.680
and so you know, for the
United States and China

00:58:46.820 --> 00:58:50.660
to make commitments of that nature,

00:58:50.660 --> 00:58:53.550
not only in terms of emissions reductions,

00:58:53.550 --> 00:58:58.280
as the two largest economies,
as the two major emitters

00:58:58.280 --> 00:59:00.230
but also for the United States to make

00:59:00.230 --> 00:59:03.300
such a substantial commitment
on the funding side

00:59:03.300 --> 00:59:06.190
for those countries that are
already feeling the effects

00:59:06.190 --> 00:59:08.580
of climate change and
there's just the cost,

00:59:08.580 --> 00:59:12.330
and I hear this from small
island countries at the UN

00:59:12.330 --> 00:59:15.570
is it's as poignant as hearing about

00:59:15.570 --> 00:59:17.646
abuses carried out in peacekeeping.

00:59:17.646 --> 00:59:19.410
I mean it's just hearing
whole villages disappear,

00:59:19.410 --> 00:59:21.820
livelihoods eradicated and so forth.

00:59:21.820 --> 00:59:26.820
So we attempted to bring
that momentum into Peru.

00:59:29.270 --> 00:59:30.530
I'm not going to make any predictions

00:59:30.530 --> 00:59:34.530
because these conferences
are very hard to predict

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:37.440
but we have a number of bites
at the apple between now

00:59:37.440 --> 00:59:39.875
and you know, the ultimate goal

00:59:39.875 --> 00:59:42.930
for something more comprehensive.

00:59:42.930 --> 00:59:43.763
Thank you.

00:59:45.535 --> 00:59:46.510
- [Audience Member] Well
first I'd like to thank you

00:59:46.510 --> 00:59:48.150
for giving such a convincing argument

00:59:48.150 --> 00:59:49.900
for why the United
States should play a role

00:59:49.900 --> 00:59:52.300
in supporting liberty
and freedom in the world,

00:59:52.300 --> 00:59:53.880
but my question is that
you talked a lot about

00:59:53.880 --> 00:59:55.900
the, like, if not for tests

00:59:55.900 --> 00:59:57.870
and evaluating humanitarian interventions,

00:59:57.870 --> 01:00:00.550
and a lot of these wars like Iraq,

01:00:00.550 --> 01:00:01.500
and some of the other ones

01:00:01.500 --> 01:00:02.640
have been billed as humanitarian,

01:00:02.640 --> 01:00:05.050
have given humanitarianism as a bad name,

01:00:05.050 --> 01:00:08.840
and have made the American
people understandably frustrated

01:00:08.840 --> 01:00:11.820
with you know, excursions,
foreign excursions.

01:00:11.820 --> 01:00:15.520
So can you explain it
how you think, well you,

01:00:15.520 --> 01:00:16.980
and the government in general,

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:19.150
or the Obama Administration
or future administrations

01:00:19.150 --> 01:00:21.663
can convince people or
convince the American people

01:00:21.663 --> 01:00:24.700
that the United States has
a role to play in the world

01:00:24.700 --> 01:00:26.950
beyond just protecting
its national security.

01:00:28.270 --> 01:00:31.770
- Well, I think first of all we have to

01:00:35.010 --> 01:00:36.833
pull apart the like and the unlike,

01:00:36.833 --> 01:00:39.400
and it's very challenging to do this,

01:00:39.400 --> 01:00:44.400
but you know, try to distinguish

01:00:45.630 --> 01:00:48.580
one particular set of
circumstances from another.

01:00:48.580 --> 01:00:52.420
So for instance the
circumstances that existed

01:00:52.420 --> 01:00:55.190
prior to the war in Iraq back in 2003

01:00:57.110 --> 01:01:00.593
were ones that President
Obama then, you know,

01:01:01.430 --> 01:01:02.480
of course not president,

01:01:02.480 --> 01:01:05.040
but nonetheless, did not find convincing

01:01:05.040 --> 01:01:09.610
as warranting the deployment of US forces

01:01:09.610 --> 01:01:13.420
and an intervention without
Security Council authorization

01:01:13.420 --> 01:01:18.420
and a lot of the sort of foreseen negative

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:23.010
international reaction which I believe,

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:24.000
as he put it at the time,

01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:26.080
was going to have
implications on the back end

01:01:26.080 --> 01:01:28.450
also for how much support one would get

01:01:28.450 --> 01:01:31.090
in sort of seeing through the mission.

01:01:31.090 --> 01:01:33.570
That is a very different
set of circumstances

01:01:33.570 --> 01:01:37.340
from the set of circumstances where ISIL,

01:01:37.340 --> 01:01:40.190
a group with, that makes very explicit

01:01:40.190 --> 01:01:45.190
its genocidal intention toward
Shia, toward Christians,

01:01:46.010 --> 01:01:48.650
toward Yazidi, forcing people to convert,

01:01:48.650 --> 01:01:51.390
raping people, enslaving people,

01:01:51.390 --> 01:01:53.720
executing American
journalists who they know

01:01:53.720 --> 01:01:56.340
have done no harm other than to try

01:01:56.340 --> 01:01:58.883
and document the suffering
of the Syrian people,

01:01:59.950 --> 01:02:02.280
so drawing those distinctions,

01:02:02.280 --> 01:02:05.890
yes it turns out there
are military operations

01:02:05.890 --> 01:02:08.200
in both instances in Iraq

01:02:08.200 --> 01:02:09.800
but there are also substantial difference

01:02:09.800 --> 01:02:13.300
in terms of the the imminent threat

01:02:13.300 --> 01:02:17.250
posed by in the case of
the the current effort

01:02:17.250 --> 01:02:20.500
by you know, something that
we haven't seen before,

01:02:20.500 --> 01:02:22.120
which is a terrorist movement

01:02:22.120 --> 01:02:25.000
controlling large swaths of territory

01:02:25.000 --> 01:02:28.140
and massive heavy weaponry.

01:02:28.140 --> 01:02:31.110
You know, much of which
it, and oil fields, right?

01:02:31.110 --> 01:02:34.100
I mean, so it's again, just to to isolate

01:02:34.100 --> 01:02:37.360
both the intentions, the actions,

01:02:37.360 --> 01:02:39.930
and the resources and
capabilities of this movement

01:02:39.930 --> 01:02:41.540
and make the case on those grounds

01:02:41.540 --> 01:02:46.540
and I think notwithstanding
the very, very understandable

01:02:48.050 --> 01:02:50.050
fatigue in this country
from being involved

01:02:50.050 --> 01:02:52.963
in war for so long in
Iraq and Afghanistan,

01:02:52.963 --> 01:02:57.963
the public support for
this effort against ISIL

01:02:59.130 --> 01:03:02.700
I think speaks to the fact
that that distinction was drawn

01:03:02.700 --> 01:03:03.900
and that people accept that these

01:03:03.900 --> 01:03:05.760
are two different circumstances.

01:03:05.760 --> 01:03:07.970
It doesn't mean that they're not concerned

01:03:07.970 --> 01:03:08.980
or that they don't raise issues

01:03:08.980 --> 01:03:10.720
that have to be taken very, very seriously

01:03:10.720 --> 01:03:13.720
about you know, ending
up in a different place

01:03:13.720 --> 01:03:18.200
but in terms of this
again, campaign in support

01:03:18.200 --> 01:03:23.200
of Iraqi efforts to to
rollback ISIL's gains

01:03:24.010 --> 01:03:25.840
and ultimately degrade and defeat ISIL

01:03:25.840 --> 01:03:27.920
there's a lot of support.

01:03:27.920 --> 01:03:28.970
The other thing we need to do though

01:03:28.970 --> 01:03:32.570
that I tried to do a little bit tonight

01:03:32.570 --> 01:03:34.110
but we need to do more of

01:03:35.270 --> 01:03:38.360
is when something is working,

01:03:38.360 --> 01:03:42.180
as for instance our intervention
in Liberia is working,

01:03:42.180 --> 01:03:46.380
it's not succeeded, it's not
close to having succeeded,

01:03:46.380 --> 01:03:50.220
but it is having measurable
demonstrable effects

01:03:50.220 --> 01:03:52.600
for families in Liberia

01:03:52.600 --> 01:03:56.380
and ultimately having
important effects also

01:03:56.380 --> 01:03:58.230
for the security the American people.

01:03:59.090 --> 01:04:03.520
We need to be sure that
we are communicating

01:04:03.520 --> 01:04:05.703
that effectively to the American people.

01:04:06.630 --> 01:04:11.630
You know it is, it is
unfortunate, somebody who has

01:04:11.990 --> 01:04:14.760
maybe some bandwidth to
do this can look at this

01:04:14.760 --> 01:04:17.500
but you know, the amount of coverage

01:04:17.500 --> 01:04:22.500
that the Ebola epidemic
got in the period where,

01:04:22.600 --> 01:04:26.180
before our interventions in
Liberia started to take effect

01:04:26.180 --> 01:04:28.780
versus the amount of coverage that,

01:04:28.780 --> 01:04:29.820
I mean, again it's normal

01:04:29.820 --> 01:04:33.050
but it's not desirable from the standpoint

01:04:33.050 --> 01:04:35.280
of the American people actually knowing

01:04:35.280 --> 01:04:37.330
the return on the investment
that they are getting

01:04:37.330 --> 01:04:39.720
by virtue of what our heroic forces

01:04:39.720 --> 01:04:43.200
and CDC epidemiologists, and aid workers,

01:04:43.200 --> 01:04:44.300
and diplomats are doing.

01:04:44.300 --> 01:04:46.320
I mean, so we have to tell that story

01:04:46.320 --> 01:04:49.100
and I try to do that wherever I can

01:04:49.100 --> 01:04:50.850
but that's a part I think of

01:04:52.920 --> 01:04:55.410
ensuring that the American
people have confidence,

01:04:55.410 --> 01:04:56.730
not only in the that we have

01:04:56.730 --> 01:04:58.470
the right objective as the government,

01:04:58.470 --> 01:05:01.610
but also that we have
the ability to achieve

01:05:01.610 --> 01:05:03.830
the objectives that we lay out,

01:05:03.830 --> 01:05:06.030
and that that confidence
is an important part

01:05:06.030 --> 01:05:08.180
of sustaining our
international engagement.

01:05:09.500 --> 01:05:11.310
- Okay I'm going to
apologize for the folks

01:05:11.310 --> 01:05:12.470
that have been standing in line

01:05:12.470 --> 01:05:13.600
because I think we're going to be able

01:05:13.600 --> 01:05:15.638
to do two more questions so--

01:05:15.638 --> 01:05:17.170
- You know, why don't
we hear from everyone

01:05:17.170 --> 01:05:18.530
and I'll just take a couple of them

01:05:18.530 --> 01:05:21.160
and at least your fellow audience members

01:05:21.160 --> 01:05:22.210
will have heard from them.

01:05:22.210 --> 01:05:24.498
- All right, rapid-fire.
- Rapid-fire.

01:05:24.498 --> 01:05:25.331
(audience laughing)

01:05:25.331 --> 01:05:26.333
- [Audience Member] Oh God, no pressure.

01:05:26.333 --> 01:05:27.166
(Samantha laughs)

01:05:27.166 --> 01:05:29.620
So, you spoke a little bit
about how important it is

01:05:29.620 --> 01:05:32.410
for the United States to
partner with other nations

01:05:32.410 --> 01:05:33.694
to promote peace and security.

01:05:33.694 --> 01:05:38.694
How do you deal with the
challenge of balancing

01:05:38.700 --> 01:05:41.170
trying to cooperate with
countries like Russia or China

01:05:41.170 --> 01:05:43.240
that we agree with on some
issues and want to work with

01:05:43.240 --> 01:05:45.710
but then on the issue
of Syria or in Ukraine

01:05:45.710 --> 01:05:47.820
or the conflicts in the South China Sea

01:05:47.820 --> 01:05:52.453
we diverge, how do you balance
those two different issues?

01:05:54.800 --> 01:05:55.633
- Yes.
- Oh.

01:05:57.110 --> 01:06:00.830
After hearing you talk about the obstacles

01:06:00.830 --> 01:06:03.500
to women's education outside the US

01:06:03.500 --> 01:06:05.810
I can't help but just feel amazingly lucky

01:06:05.810 --> 01:06:08.170
to get to go to school, you know, at all

01:06:08.170 --> 01:06:09.450
and especially at a place like this

01:06:09.450 --> 01:06:11.420
and I'm wondering if, you know,

01:06:11.420 --> 01:06:13.010
given all the experiential learning

01:06:13.010 --> 01:06:14.280
that has happened for you

01:06:14.280 --> 01:06:17.140
between the time you graduated
Yale and where you are now

01:06:17.140 --> 01:06:19.100
is there anything you learned here

01:06:19.100 --> 01:06:21.530
either academically or socially

01:06:21.530 --> 01:06:24.130
that's really made an impact
on how you do your job?

01:06:25.330 --> 01:06:26.710
- [Audience Member] I'm
wondering if you can comment

01:06:26.710 --> 01:06:28.590
on the future of the responsibility

01:06:28.590 --> 01:06:31.850
to protect doctrine given the challenges

01:06:31.850 --> 01:06:33.373
of intervening in Syria?

01:06:36.530 --> 01:06:39.050
- [Audience Member] Two great
historical events in my life,

01:06:39.050 --> 01:06:42.030
when I met my wife,
that was the number one,

01:06:42.030 --> 01:06:45.370
and number two, the formation
of the United Nations.

01:06:45.370 --> 01:06:49.520
Can you give us some
idea of what's going on

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:54.520
to make the United
Nations a more efficient

01:06:54.690 --> 01:06:59.613
and organization in terms
of reforms of various types?

01:07:01.650 --> 01:07:02.590
- [Audience Member] I
appreciated your words

01:07:02.590 --> 01:07:05.350
about global threats
requiring global cooperation

01:07:05.350 --> 01:07:07.160
in the context of Ebola

01:07:07.160 --> 01:07:09.950
or fighting the threat from
returning foreign fighters

01:07:09.950 --> 01:07:11.550
and I'm wondering what you think

01:07:11.550 --> 01:07:14.740
in the next few decades
will be the challenges,

01:07:14.740 --> 01:07:18.130
limits, and benefits of
empowering regional partnerships,

01:07:18.130 --> 01:07:23.130
not just in the US and India
or direct bilateral relations

01:07:23.240 --> 01:07:24.924
for things like the climate deal

01:07:24.924 --> 01:07:27.130
but directly empowering regional powers

01:07:27.130 --> 01:07:30.080
to be presences in their regions

01:07:30.080 --> 01:07:32.480
and to deal with some of the and issues

01:07:32.480 --> 01:07:35.130
and guide in ways that
the US may not be able to

01:07:35.130 --> 01:07:37.223
or willing to in the next coming decades?

01:07:38.440 --> 01:07:39.750
- [Audience Member] First of
all I wanted to say thank you

01:07:39.750 --> 01:07:41.230
'cause I know you're a
big inspiration to me

01:07:41.230 --> 01:07:43.160
and probably many in this room.

01:07:43.160 --> 01:07:44.930
I've heard World Fellow Rami Nakhla,

01:07:44.930 --> 01:07:47.840
who's an activist in
Syria speak frequently

01:07:47.840 --> 01:07:50.980
this semester about the
nature of the ISIS problem

01:07:50.980 --> 01:07:53.070
being not just something
that's a short-term solution

01:07:53.070 --> 01:07:54.300
but as you said long game

01:07:54.300 --> 01:07:56.500
and something that relates very strongly

01:07:56.500 --> 01:07:57.800
to the power vacuum in Syria.

01:07:57.800 --> 01:07:59.860
So I was wondering if
you could talk more about

01:07:59.860 --> 01:08:01.670
what the frontier is on that issue

01:08:01.670 --> 01:08:04.010
because most of what I've
heard is very cynical

01:08:04.010 --> 01:08:05.550
and that it's just
something that's going on

01:08:05.550 --> 01:08:07.440
but I know that you've written obviously

01:08:07.440 --> 01:08:09.380
about how America responds
in the age of genocide

01:08:09.380 --> 01:08:11.180
and what you think the next step is?

01:08:12.930 --> 01:08:14.880
- [Audience Member] So you
spoke a little bit about

01:08:14.880 --> 01:08:17.920
how Islam does not support
the actions of ISIS

01:08:18.770 --> 01:08:22.210
and so I was wondering why
we're still calling it ISIS

01:08:22.210 --> 01:08:25.530
and rewarding it by
branding it with the name,

01:08:25.530 --> 01:08:30.270
you know, of a religion
that supports, you know,

01:08:30.270 --> 01:08:32.980
that's a good religion with good people.

01:08:32.980 --> 01:08:34.683
So I just, yeah, thank you.

01:08:36.220 --> 01:08:37.170
- [Audience Member] Ambassador
thank you for being--

01:08:37.170 --> 01:08:39.320
- They would have us
call it Islamic State,

01:08:40.480 --> 01:08:41.800
they would have us call it,

01:08:41.800 --> 01:08:45.443
I mean that is their their
current title for themselves

01:08:45.443 --> 01:08:47.650
but anyway go ahead, sorry.

01:08:47.650 --> 01:08:49.070
- [Audience Member] With the
Millennium Development Goals

01:08:49.070 --> 01:08:51.090
set to expire and the
Sustainable Development Goals

01:08:51.090 --> 01:08:52.380
being next on the agenda,

01:08:52.380 --> 01:08:53.340
I'm wondering what you see

01:08:53.340 --> 01:08:55.660
as the greatest challenge
in those negotiations

01:08:55.660 --> 01:08:57.360
and which countries the United States

01:08:57.360 --> 01:09:00.583
is going to need to engage
to achieve its objectives.

01:09:03.440 --> 01:09:05.380
- [Audience Member] You so much
for being here, Ambassador.

01:09:05.380 --> 01:09:08.520
Given that 2015 is the 100th anniversary

01:09:08.520 --> 01:09:12.970
of the Armenian Genocide, I'm
wondering what your feelings

01:09:12.970 --> 01:09:15.940
are on how the UN will approach that

01:09:15.940 --> 01:09:18.640
and whether this will
be a time to bring up

01:09:18.640 --> 01:09:22.240
more or less publicity
about genocide in general

01:09:22.240 --> 01:09:26.060
and whether this anniversary is

01:09:26.060 --> 01:09:29.630
an important thing for
Americans to also be aware of,

01:09:29.630 --> 01:09:32.440
given that there's a
large Armenian population

01:09:32.440 --> 01:09:34.850
but also given the many other genocides

01:09:34.850 --> 01:09:36.600
that have happened since that time.

01:09:38.340 --> 01:09:39.370
- [Audience Member] Hi, my question

01:09:39.370 --> 01:09:41.233
is more about your background.

01:09:42.130 --> 01:09:43.750
You were a journalist

01:09:43.750 --> 01:09:48.750
and you were, had just graduated from Yale

01:09:49.400 --> 01:09:51.740
when the Yugoslavian genocide began,

01:09:51.740 --> 01:09:53.200
you saw the Rwandan genocide,

01:09:53.200 --> 01:09:55.990
and you saw the UN system fail,

01:09:55.990 --> 01:09:57.980
and now as being part of that system

01:09:57.980 --> 01:10:01.910
how does that inform your role in the UN,

01:10:01.910 --> 01:10:03.480
particularly with intervention?

01:10:03.480 --> 01:10:05.340
- Mm-hmm.

01:10:05.340 --> 01:10:06.530
- [Audience Member] Thank
you so much for being here.

01:10:06.530 --> 01:10:09.470
My own studies have focused
on gender promotion in Rwanda

01:10:09.470 --> 01:10:11.770
which have been to a degree successful

01:10:11.770 --> 01:10:13.440
through a combination
of the country's size,

01:10:13.440 --> 01:10:14.680
political well,

01:10:14.680 --> 01:10:16.520
and the fact that it's post-conflict.

01:10:16.520 --> 01:10:18.840
Of course you look at places like the DRC

01:10:18.840 --> 01:10:22.070
where violence and conflict
themselves have become endemic.

01:10:22.070 --> 01:10:25.030
How do you combat kind
of gender-based violence

01:10:25.030 --> 01:10:26.650
in those situations?

01:10:26.650 --> 01:10:27.483
Thank you.

01:10:29.220 --> 01:10:32.010
- [Audience Member] My question
is about genocide also.

01:10:32.010 --> 01:10:35.060
I count probably two major instances

01:10:35.060 --> 01:10:36.260
in the current administration

01:10:36.260 --> 01:10:38.470
of the word genocide
being used very publicly

01:10:38.470 --> 01:10:41.070
and very impactfully in Benghazi

01:10:41.070 --> 01:10:45.730
and then earlier this year
surrounding the Yazidis in Iraq

01:10:45.730 --> 01:10:48.420
and I would like you to just maybe briefly

01:10:48.420 --> 01:10:51.600
give a retrospective grading on the

01:10:51.600 --> 01:10:53.730
administration's use of that term

01:10:53.730 --> 01:10:58.730
and whether it kind of fits in the model

01:11:00.340 --> 01:11:04.110
that you would think is appropriate
for the use of that term

01:11:04.110 --> 01:11:07.090
and whether or not you
think it's maybe broader

01:11:07.090 --> 01:11:09.340
than just one president
or one administration

01:11:09.340 --> 01:11:13.080
and if it reflects a
broader institutional change

01:11:13.080 --> 01:11:14.080
by the US Government

01:11:14.080 --> 01:11:15.560
and the way it uses that term

01:11:15.560 --> 01:11:19.510
and how that term is used to affect

01:11:19.510 --> 01:11:20.500
broader action in the world?

01:11:20.500 --> 01:11:22.013
Thank you.

01:11:22.013 --> 01:11:23.280
- One more.
_ Thank you, Ambassador.

01:11:23.280 --> 01:11:25.680
I just want a question, how can the US

01:11:25.680 --> 01:11:27.240
alongside the United Nations

01:11:27.240 --> 01:11:32.240
block any future military,
kind of, I would say a war

01:11:34.170 --> 01:11:36.840
kind of, between the Israelis
and the Palestinians.

01:11:36.840 --> 01:11:40.080
How can we block a future
Israeli raid on Gaza

01:11:40.080 --> 01:11:42.540
and killing civilians, thank you.

01:11:42.540 --> 01:11:44.340
- So Ambassador Power,

01:11:44.340 --> 01:11:46.080
I'm gonna be deeply impressed by the way

01:11:46.080 --> 01:11:48.902
that you pulled this off.
- No, you won't be.

01:11:48.902 --> 01:11:51.229
- And I know that in advance

01:11:51.229 --> 01:11:54.340
and also I think we've
got enough material here

01:11:54.340 --> 01:11:56.094
for another visit next
week, don't you think?

01:11:56.094 --> 01:11:57.590
(audience laughing)

01:11:57.590 --> 01:12:00.800
- Okay first let me say one reason

01:12:00.800 --> 01:12:02.150
I think that was the right thing to do

01:12:02.150 --> 01:12:04.223
is just what an amazing,

01:12:05.730 --> 01:12:07.430
what an amazing group of students,

01:12:08.939 --> 01:12:12.238
definitely the standards have
gone up since they let me in.

01:12:12.238 --> 01:12:13.200
(audience laughing)

01:12:13.200 --> 01:12:15.480
So there's not one of those questions

01:12:15.480 --> 01:12:17.600
I would have been capable of asking

01:12:19.180 --> 01:12:20.980
when I was here as an
undergrad but nonetheless,

01:12:20.980 --> 01:12:23.883
now I'm stuck answering them.

01:12:24.810 --> 01:12:25.810
So let me,

01:12:25.810 --> 01:12:27.330
what I think I'll do is just,

01:12:27.330 --> 01:12:30.480
I'm actually not gonna try
to tick on each of them

01:12:30.480 --> 01:12:32.210
but I will, I want to just say a few words

01:12:32.210 --> 01:12:34.580
about the issues that
haven't come up yet so far

01:12:34.580 --> 01:12:36.970
and then just encourage those students,

01:12:36.970 --> 01:12:39.400
I don't recall exactly
where we're going next

01:12:39.400 --> 01:12:42.000
but we have some next on
the Grateful Dead Tour,

01:12:42.000 --> 01:12:44.570
we're making our way to the next venue,

01:12:44.570 --> 01:12:46.320
and then people who don't feel

01:12:46.320 --> 01:12:47.630
I was responsive to their question

01:12:47.630 --> 01:12:49.270
I'm more than happy to engage,

01:12:49.270 --> 01:12:52.610
but one because I think it's a question,

01:12:52.610 --> 01:12:56.250
the very first question one about

01:12:56.250 --> 01:13:01.040
how we continue to work
everyday with Russia,

01:13:01.040 --> 01:13:02.990
notwithstanding Ukraine, and Syria,

01:13:02.990 --> 01:13:05.003
and these very divisive issues.

01:13:06.630 --> 01:13:07.490
I think it's just a question

01:13:07.490 --> 01:13:08.730
that's been on a lot of people's minds

01:13:08.730 --> 01:13:10.383
so I'll take that question.

01:13:13.120 --> 01:13:17.490
I think the answer is that each of us,

01:13:17.490 --> 01:13:21.280
that the United States and
Russia each have interests

01:13:21.280 --> 01:13:24.943
that we want to see advanced in Syria.

01:13:27.210 --> 01:13:29.810
For instance, our interests in,

01:13:29.810 --> 01:13:31.330
that are very much overlapping,

01:13:31.330 --> 01:13:33.400
should be overlapping with Russia's,

01:13:33.400 --> 01:13:37.740
preventing foreign
terrorist fighters flow,

01:13:37.740 --> 01:13:41.290
preventing terrorist groups
from gaining a foothold,

01:13:41.290 --> 01:13:43.510
preventing chemical weapons use,

01:13:43.510 --> 01:13:44.343
those are our interests

01:13:44.343 --> 01:13:48.040
and those are why we have
taken the policy positions

01:13:48.040 --> 01:13:51.540
that we have, very much alongside
preventing the slaughter

01:13:51.540 --> 01:13:54.090
of innocent families, which
is happening every day.

01:13:56.390 --> 01:14:01.390
Russia has at least some
of those shared interests

01:14:01.550 --> 01:14:04.720
but their route to
advancing those interests

01:14:04.720 --> 01:14:07.210
is very distinct from ours

01:14:07.210 --> 01:14:10.117
and that is what creates
the clash that we have seen

01:14:10.117 --> 01:14:11.943
and the vetoes that I've mentioned.

01:14:12.830 --> 01:14:15.890
In other words they
view Assad as the route

01:14:15.890 --> 01:14:19.530
to achieving many of the
objectives that I've described

01:14:19.530 --> 01:14:24.360
and sort of just accepting
his rule in perpetuity

01:14:24.360 --> 01:14:26.470
as being in our collective interests,

01:14:26.470 --> 01:14:27.970
which they would characterize,

01:14:29.140 --> 01:14:32.830
but at the same time we are in gridlock

01:14:32.830 --> 01:14:35.330
in the Security Council over Syria.

01:14:35.330 --> 01:14:40.270
We are working side by side
in the P5+1 negotiations

01:14:40.270 --> 01:14:45.030
to try to come up with
a comprehensive deal

01:14:45.030 --> 01:14:48.850
that would deny Iran a
pathway to a nuclear weapon

01:14:49.860 --> 01:14:53.810
and Russia has participated
constructively in that.

01:14:53.810 --> 01:14:57.570
By the same token on
many, many Africa issues,

01:14:57.570 --> 01:15:00.180
whether South Sudan or the
Central African Republic,

01:15:00.180 --> 01:15:04.470
or Mali, you know, they, you know,

01:15:04.470 --> 01:15:07.200
issues where their immediate
interests may not be at stake

01:15:07.200 --> 01:15:12.200
but they still vote in support
of peacekeeping operations

01:15:15.020 --> 01:15:18.160
or in support even of sanctions
designations and so forth.

01:15:18.160 --> 01:15:23.160
So there's a fair amount
of cooperation on issues

01:15:25.110 --> 01:15:29.260
not related to Syria and
Ukraine that goes on in New York

01:15:29.260 --> 01:15:31.610
and it's very useful for our interests

01:15:31.610 --> 01:15:33.070
that that is the case.

01:15:33.070 --> 01:15:36.610
Our cooperation the Security
Council will never lead us

01:15:36.610 --> 01:15:38.930
to pull our punches however on

01:15:38.930 --> 01:15:41.150
human rights abuses inside Russia

01:15:42.030 --> 01:15:47.030
and the kind of over brazen aggression

01:15:48.610 --> 01:15:50.840
that we've seen in Ukraine.

01:15:50.840 --> 01:15:54.053
So there are tensions in all
of that, as you can imagine.

01:15:55.884 --> 01:15:59.410
In terms of just flagging
a couple other things

01:15:59.410 --> 01:16:00.410
that hadn't come up.

01:16:01.570 --> 01:16:03.900
I think the Millennium
Development Goals is something

01:16:03.900 --> 01:16:07.260
a lot of students on
campus were interested in

01:16:07.260 --> 01:16:08.810
and got a lot of traction,

01:16:08.810 --> 01:16:13.010
these goals for kids being
in school, for malaria,

01:16:13.010 --> 01:16:15.830
for you know, a whole host
of social and economic goods

01:16:15.830 --> 01:16:18.240
and they've had a stickiness
particularly in the health area

01:16:18.240 --> 01:16:20.190
that has been very, very important.

01:16:20.190 --> 01:16:23.390
Our biggest challenge in
negotiating the successor goals

01:16:23.390 --> 01:16:27.371
to the MDGs is the success of the MDGs

01:16:27.371 --> 01:16:32.250
because these goals became actionable

01:16:32.250 --> 01:16:35.240
and resources have been
deployed around a set of goals,

01:16:35.240 --> 01:16:38.520
measurable targets,

01:16:38.520 --> 01:16:40.620
if you have an issue that matters to you

01:16:41.530 --> 01:16:42.820
and you're in this negotiation,

01:16:42.820 --> 01:16:45.090
you want that issue represented

01:16:45.090 --> 01:16:46.520
in the new set of goals

01:16:46.520 --> 01:16:48.510
because you believe that it will mean

01:16:48.510 --> 01:16:50.770
that you get the resources that you need

01:16:50.770 --> 01:16:55.100
and every country is bringing
this kind of attitude to it.

01:16:55.100 --> 01:16:58.300
In other words the stakes
are seen as extremely high

01:16:58.300 --> 01:17:03.300
but the results right now
is a very, very long list

01:17:03.590 --> 01:17:07.283
and he who fights every
battle risks fighting none,

01:17:08.300 --> 01:17:10.040
so the biggest challenge is

01:17:10.040 --> 01:17:12.840
because of the success
perversely of the MDGs

01:17:12.840 --> 01:17:16.473
and the faith that people have
injected in this new process,

01:17:19.050 --> 01:17:20.640
it will be challenging, very challenging

01:17:20.640 --> 01:17:22.630
to discipline the list.

01:17:22.630 --> 01:17:25.530
The last thing I'd say just
in terms of what I learned

01:17:25.530 --> 01:17:30.083
academically or socially, nothing
socially, that's for sure,

01:17:33.108 --> 01:17:36.608
but I would say, I mean, the amazing thing

01:17:38.600 --> 01:17:42.450
about being at Yale was opening up,

01:17:42.450 --> 01:17:43.890
we used to have this thing,

01:17:43.890 --> 01:17:45.780
this is ridiculous that
this is no longer the case,

01:17:45.780 --> 01:17:47.770
but we used to have the Blue Book

01:17:47.770 --> 01:17:49.550
and opening up the Blue Book--

01:17:49.550 --> 01:17:50.460
- [Jeff] It's still there.

01:17:50.460 --> 01:17:52.020
- Yeah but it's online, doesn't count,

01:17:52.020 --> 01:17:53.590
it's nowhere near the same.

01:17:53.590 --> 01:17:55.220
It was just this, it would come

01:17:55.220 --> 01:17:59.720
and it was this amazing
euphoric experience

01:17:59.720 --> 01:18:02.740
where your eyes were so
much bigger than your tummy

01:18:02.740 --> 01:18:05.840
and you wanted to take
everything and learn everything

01:18:05.840 --> 01:18:09.700
and inject yourself into
a whole host of domains

01:18:09.700 --> 01:18:12.560
that you had no business
being in, in some cases

01:18:13.710 --> 01:18:15.680
but I think that's the spirit,

01:18:15.680 --> 01:18:18.840
that spirit of believing that you can

01:18:18.840 --> 01:18:22.840
both learn, you know, which
can be a burden, it's hard,

01:18:22.840 --> 01:18:25.830
particularly in competitive
environments like this one

01:18:25.830 --> 01:18:28.060
and love it.

01:18:28.060 --> 01:18:30.650
I think that is the thing
that I took from here

01:18:30.650 --> 01:18:33.340
and I've just been extremely privileged

01:18:33.340 --> 01:18:37.060
in my professional career
to constantly learn

01:18:37.060 --> 01:18:38.590
and also just love it

01:18:38.590 --> 01:18:41.230
and bring that expectation every day that

01:18:41.230 --> 01:18:42.940
we can be inspired by our work

01:18:42.940 --> 01:18:45.190
and even if we're not succeeding always

01:18:45.190 --> 01:18:46.380
in just the way we like

01:18:48.120 --> 01:18:50.270
that to have the privilege of trying

01:18:50.270 --> 01:18:53.360
is you know, just a great, great blessing.

01:18:53.360 --> 01:18:58.190
So the Blue Book spirit
that I take to my day job,

01:18:58.190 --> 01:19:00.120
I think is what I would share.

01:19:00.120 --> 01:19:01.294
Anyway with that.

01:19:01.294 --> 01:19:02.127
- [Jeff] Thank you, very much.

01:19:02.127 --> 01:19:02.960
- Thank you.

01:19:02.960 --> 01:19:05.989
(audience applauding)

01:19:05.989 --> 01:19:08.658
- Well once we get out here,
we can get on a schedule.

01:19:08.658 --> 01:19:10.066
- I think if we do.

01:19:10.066 --> 01:19:12.733
(gentle music)

